window.harnessChunks["rows-a96b0144a7187c44.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-06T17:09:15+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/echoVic/blade-code\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Blade Code</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-06\">2026-10-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/echoVic/blade-code/tree/30f868411df4f7da095aadb7a2a8ca3310315613\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 30f868411df4f7da095aadb7a2a8ca3310315613\">30f8684 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform software-engineering and automation work through model-selected repository/tool actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar code, implementation choices, tool/test/browser failures, model/provider variation, context pressure and bounded delegated work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive coding/tool actions, interpret returned evidence, decide when to delegate or create a team and decide when the assigned operational objective is complete. - Decision owner: the active model-backed Blade Code agent and, for delegated bounded work, first-party subagent/team-member agents. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry/executor, permissions, context/session runtime, provider adapters, task admission, goal state, worktrees and cancellation. - Closure path: user/parent goal → agent chooses direct or delegated coding action → first-party runtime executes → tool/subagent/team evidence returns → agent revises work → completion is accepted when runtime completion gates permit it. - Boundary reachability: ordinary CLI/Web/Headless/ACP runtime uses the same first-party Agent/tool machinery; Task and Team tools are shipped model-callable surfaces. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic code executes and constrains actions, but the model chooses open-ended implementation, investigation, delegation and completion decisions. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/agent/Agent.ts; packages/cli/src/tools/builtin/task/task.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: permissions and scheduling can delay/block actions without becoming the owner of the engineering decision itself.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate interference among concurrent operational teammates by assigning dependencies/ownership and isolating write-capable workstreams while coordinating peer progress. - Disturbance / variety regulated: duplicate task claiming, dependency-order violations, concurrent workers editing overlapping workspace state, and peer work that must be synchronized before downstream tasks proceed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the team members, task partition, dependency graph, initial assignments and whether work benefits from parallel specialization/peer review; use peer messages to adjust coordination as execution unfolds. - Decision owner: the model-backed team lead through TeamCreate/SendMessage, with deterministic task-graph/worktree mechanisms enforcing the selected relation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TeamTaskGraph, atomic claim, automatic unblock, durable peer mailbox, managed worktree isolation for write-capable members and worktree lifecycle/merge machinery. - Closure path: lead identifies separable/interdependent work → creates roles/tasks/dependencies → teammates claim only unblocked work and write-capable members default to isolated worktrees → completion/unblocking/messages update peer execution → downstream units proceed under the adjusted coordination state. - Boundary reachability: TeamCreate, TeamTaskClaim, SendMessage and managed teammate worktrees are shipped first-party runtime tools, not documentation-only concepts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime enforces claims, dependencies and isolation, but the lead agent chooses the substantive organizational partition and dependency structure. - Distinct S1 units: persistent model-backed teammates/subagents that own bounded implementation or review outcomes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent workers can duplicate work, violate ordering, or collide through shared writes; managed worktrees and dependency/claim rules exist specifically to reduce those interactions. - Attenuating coordination relation: lead-selected DAG/assignment plus atomic task claiming, automatic dependency unblocking, durable peer messaging and worktree isolation for writers. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: completed tasks unblock dependent work; peer messages enter running teammate turns; task ownership/claim state changes which unit may work next; isolated results are later integrated rather than concurrently overwriting the same checkout. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the mechanisms regulate concrete interaction among simultaneously viable operational workers rather than merely transporting a delegated prompt. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/tools/builtin/team/teamTools.ts; packages/cli/src/agent/teams/TeamTaskGraph.ts; packages/cli/src/agent/subagents/SubagentWorktreeLifecycle.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: atomic claiming and worktree mechanics are deterministic support; autonomy is credited to the lead&#39;s model-owned organizational decisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a live whole-team view and regulate current operational commitments while the multi-agent organization is running. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled or unwanted teammates, changed current priorities, task/member status changes, blocked downstream work and a team that should be stopped after synthesis or failure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the live team/member/task graph, steer a running teammate with a direct message, broadcast a changed instruction, and terminate running teammates by deleting the current team. - Decision owner: the model-backed team lead through TeamStatus, SendMessage and TeamDelete. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentSessionStore-backed live member state, durable task graph, TeamMailbox, running-turn message delivery and team/member cancellation. - Closure path: lead inspects TeamStatus/current graph → detects a current execution need → sends targeted/broadcast steering or terminates the team → running teammate/session state changes → later TeamStatus/results reflect the intervention and the lead proceeds accordingly. - Boundary reachability: TeamStatus/SendMessage/TeamDelete are shipped model-callable first-party tools available to the team lead. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: live state storage and cancellation transport are deterministic, but the lead model interprets whole-current state and decides the substantive intervention. - Whole-system current view: TeamStatus returns live member state from AgentSessionStore together with durable task-graph state for the owned team. - Current-control decision scope: current peer steering, task/role supervision through the shared graph, and cancellation of running team commitments when work should stop. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/tools/builtin/team/teamTools.ts; packages/cli/src/agent/teams/TeamRuntime.ts; packages/cli/src/agent/teams/TeamMailbox.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic stop/cancel would not be enough alone; the positive claim rests on the combined live whole-team status surface plus targeted running-turn steering and commitment termination.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge qualifying implementation work with a fresh read-only verifier whose evidence can block completion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect implementation claims, failing tests/types/lint, medium/high-risk defects, race/security issues and author self-verification bias. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently execute allowed verification commands, inspect the authoritative changed-file scope and emit structured PASS/FAIL/PARTIAL findings. - Decision owner: the separately instantiated built-in verification agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh subagent context, read-only tool restrictions, authoritative verification prompt, mutation-revision tracking, bounded retry gate and deterministic completion policy. - Closure path: main agent changes qualifying implementation → runtime requires fresh verification Task → independent verifier gathers direct project/tool evidence and emits verdict → FAIL/PARTIAL forces repair/re-verification; only fresh PASS for current mutation revision permits completion. - Boundary reachability: independentVerification is wired into the shipped main loop and explicitly requires the built-in verification subagent for qualifying changes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the verifier model owns the audit judgment from its own evidence path; deterministic runtime code enforces freshness and blocks completion when the verdict is insufficient. - Claim being audited: the main coding agent&#39;s claim that the current non-trivial implementation is correct and ready to finish. - Ordinary reporting path: the author agent&#39;s normal tool results, implementation narrative and self-checks. - Complementary access path: a separate read-only verifier runs project checks and reads changed files directly under an authoritative scope. - Independence boundary: fresh model-backed subagent, no write tools, no nested subagents, tool-evidence-only instructions and no reliance on the author&#39;s claimed test outcome. - Who acts on findings: the main agent must repair reported failures and invoke a new verification run; the runtime completion gate prevents success until the required PASS is fresh. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/agent/loop/independentVerification.ts; packages/cli/src/agent/subagents/verification.md; packages/cli/src/agent/subagents/builtinVerificationAgent.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic code-review skills are not used for this claim; the positive S3 path is the enforced built-in verifier.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying future/external condition is placed under a function that develops adaptation options and returns them into current S3 capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S4. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: auto-memory, compaction-time consolidation, provider health/recovery, web/browser tools, goals, schedules and plugin/skill configuration can affect later work but do not by themselves close S4. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory consolidation extracts preferences/conventions/lessons/debugging notes and injects retained project knowledge into later sessions; that is persistence/learning context rather than an evidenced outside-and-then option-development conversation with current S3. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/memory/AutoMemoryManager.ts; packages/cli/src/memory/MemoryConsolidation.ts; packages/cli/src/goals/executionFrontier.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the runtime has substantial persistence and external tooling, but these do not satisfy the Methodology 0.3.6 external-and-prospective option loop without an adaptation owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no identity-level or ultimate-policy conflict is routed to a distinct authoritative S5 owner. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission modes, workspace trust, system prompts, tool policy, plugin/MCP security and configuration constrain execution but do not create identity-governance closure. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Blade Code agents operate within developer/user-authored constraints and do not have authority to redefine the system&#39;s ultimate identity or governing principles. - Evidence: packages/cli/src/config/PermissionChecker.ts; packages/cli/src/security/WorkspaceTrustService.ts; packages/cli/src/prompts/sections.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: operator permissions and configuration are legitimate constraints, not identity/ultimate-policy governance.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/37d2b8f891a1fec144fc3d87e36ec29a6c976a96/assessments/blade-code.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 37d2b8f891a1fec144fc3d87e36ec29a6c976a96\">37d2b8f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-11T23:52:40+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MaximeRobeyns/self_improving_coding_agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SICA</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-11\">2026-10-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MaximeRobeyns/self_improving_coding_agent/tree/ed8275dca4d3c5dbf77229964351fe9b424797dc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ed8275dca4d3c5dbf77229964351fe9b424797dc\">ed8275d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: First-party agent loops perform model-directed coding/source inspection, sandboxed shell operations, benchmark problem solving and independently executing delegated workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New bug/task descriptions, mutable repository content, tool errors, model output and local experiment conditions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model selects concrete coding steps and allowed tools after receiving tool outputs or prior subagent results. - Decision owner: Executing CodingAgent/ProblemSolver child model, MainOrchestrator&#39;s bounded solver actions and base agent tool-call continuation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent classes, tool parser and call graph, executecommand, file and edit tools, sandbox Docker process. - Closure path: Task → model-directed operations → actual tool/command outputs and artifact → subsequent step or returned result, with sandbox limits. - Boundary reachability: Packaged baseagent.agent runs MainOrchestratorAgent, which can create coding/model workers; shipped runner.py invokes that executable in isolated Docker containers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task-specific coding decisions are model-generated rather than only an external benchmark script. - Evidence: baseagent/agent.py; baseagent/src/agents/implementations/coder.py; baseagent/src/agents/agentcalling.py; baseagent/src/tools/executecommand.py; runner.py - Basis: explicit / structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent distinct concurrent benchmark-running agent S1 workers from interfering through shared host HTTP ports and shared benchmark result-tracker mutation. - Distinct S1 units: Multiple Python/Docker child coding agents each solve separate benchmark problems through runbenchmarkswithjobqueue and processjob, independently maintaining a coding task loop. - Inter-S1 disturbance: If two independently executing benchmark agents bind the same host port 8080/808x, one container fails to start; concurrent writes to the same benchmark JSONL tracker may lose or corrupt result/progress state. - Attenuating coordination relation: runbenchmarkswithjobqueue allocates a unique workerid from bounded idqueue; processjob binds container host port 808{workerid}. Each benchmark uses asyncio.Lock around tracker start/result updates, preventing simultaneous writes to shared provenance. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Each worker occupies one reserved ID/port while running its model and returns ID only on exit; subsequent agent work waits for a safe slot or starts on another allocated ID; results can be safely saved and analyzed for next iteration. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The first-party ID lease and tracker lock address two named interference modes produced by distinct simultaneously acting model agents; the evidence goes beyond mere task assignment/queue routing. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Host port contention and cross-worker result-store writes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Allocate/release exclusive host port and serialize benchmark tracker mutations. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic idqueue and per-benchmark asyncio.Lock constructor, not an autonomous S2 mediator model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bounded maxworkers, unique Docker names, per-problem workdir mounts and benchmark data lock. - Closure path: Several runnable problem jobs → lease workerid/unique host port → parallel model sessions → guarded tracker write → release lease → next job. - Boundary reachability: Default runner.py uses this first-party benchmark job runner during evaluation of each agent iteration; positive S2 is bounded to this concurrent benchmark-agent mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Safe exclusive-resource scheduling is deterministic host-owned (C), not the meta agent&#39;s discretionary choice. - Evidence: runner.py; baseagent/src/benchmarks/base.py - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Model-driven executive direction of multiple current subordinate coding workers with shared task progress and corrective redirection based on their execution results. - Whole-system current view: MainOrchestrator owns the top-level task and receives the complete problem and a synthesized live execution tree from CallGraphManager after subordinate calls, including already attempted work/results and current subordinate execution state. - Current-control decision scope: MainOrchestrator selects/decomposes active subagent tasks, assigns complete subtask instructions, scrutinizes their returned evidence, changes the plan, commissions further workers/verification and decides when a coherent current task result is submitted. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Subagent failures, incomplete work, duplicate approaches, requirement mismatch and uncertain current progress. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Discretionary model-controlled reassignment, continuation/stop and verification of the current multi-agent goal; no reliance solely on fixed queue. - Decision owner: First-party MainOrchestratorAgent LLM (A) at parent/current multiunit recursion, separate from subordinate child-model operational work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CallGraphManager, generated execution-tree event, model-driven agent calls, structured task results, cost/time governors. - Closure path: Current top-level intent → orchestrator decomposition → model worker runs → returned results plus direct callgraph operating state → revised plans/delegation and final task acceptance. - Boundary reachability: baseagent/agent.py constructs MainOrchestratorAgent and starts it as the initial executing actor in CLI and benchmark mode; role documented in the agent&#39;s first-party core prompt. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The parent model uses evidence and discretionary judgment to govern concurrent/successive independently thinking subagent units, rather than merely running a predefined list of subtasks. - Evidence: baseagent/agent.py; baseagent/src/agents/implementations/mainorchestrator.py; baseagent/src/callgraph/manager.py; baseagent/src/agents/agentcalling.py - Basis: explicit / structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Separate model-driven runtime monitor checks independent live callgraph/effect events for subagent nonprogress, looping and abnormal execution and can issue corrective notices or terminate faulty child work. - Claim being audited: Operational actors&#39; implicit assertions of continuing useful work and healthy nested execution; notably a child may be silently looping or failing while its parent awaits results. - Ordinary reporting path: Each coding child returns self-authored work and final text to its parent, potentially omitting stalled nested activity. - Complementary access path: An independently launched Overseer model periodically/event-triggeredly reads generateoverseerexecutiontree(CallGraphManager), a first-party event-derived live execution graph, not only the child/parent&#39;s narrative result. This is source-observed execution, although not an independently executed code quality benchmark. - Independence boundary: Overseer is a separate model invocation outside producer and supervising parent tool-delegation turn, with its own model option and timing/event observations. Shared first-party callgraph and provider limits remain; no external independent auditor claim. - Who acts on findings: Overseer itself publishes OVERSEERNOTIFICATION to an implicated agent/parent and calls CallGraphManager.cancelagent when model judge sets forcecancelagent; parent responds via next tool/model decision. It also adapts future monitoring cadence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task nonprogress, recursion loops, stalled/erratic worker execution and hidden operational states. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model independently decides concern/notifiability, which actor to notify, whether to cancel, and whether future checks are time/event-driven. - Decision owner: Separate autonomous Overseer model, not a rule-only timeout checker. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: EventBus subscriptions, execution-tree renderer, OverseerJudgement schema, LLM check interval, notifications and cancellation path. - Closure path: Real child event and call graph → separate model Overseer diagnosis → notification or direct cancel → parent agent control loop sees issue and can reassign; subsequent oversight resamples. - Boundary reachability: baseagent/agent.py always instantiates and starts Overseer when running packaged agent; its monitoringloop subscribes to tool result/assistant events while root agent executes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The audit&#39;s judgment and direct cancellation/intervention are separate model decisions using a complementary event-derived operating view; not a producer self-review or passive observability dashboard. - Evidence: baseagent/agent.py; baseagent/src/oversight/overseer.py; baseagent/src/callgraph/reporting.py; baseagent/src/callgraph/manager.py - Basis: explicit / structural - Confidence: medium-high for wiring, medium for audit effectiveness - Caveats: Overseer is an execution-health complementary audit, not independent code correctness review. It may share model provider and callgraph evidence with producer; cancellation can be mistaken or delayed. ReviewCommittee is separately model-driven design critique but is not credited as direct runtime correctness audit.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Model-driven future-capability adaptation: evaluate externally defined benchmark tasks and past agent-version results, generate and implement software architecture/tool changes for subsequent agent generations. - External distinction: Benchmark tasks and measured grader outcomes originate outside the current coding agent&#39;s self-report; the archive records past failures, costs and utility scores. Published literature/web exploration can inform new feature options in the meta-improvement reasoning workflow. - Future / prospective distinction: The meta agent is tasked to improve the next version&#39;s general coding competence, not just solve the currently evaluated benchmark problem or fix the present code task. - Adaptation option generated: MetaImprovementReasoningStructure directs archive analysis (working and failed features), a model-chosen new feature/strategy, code change proposal, multi-member read-only ReviewCommittee critique, model-directed implementation and planned tests. - Path back into current capability / S3: runner.py evaluates a version, selects a base version by stored benchmark statistics and confidence bound, copies code into next iteration, runs that version&#39;s model-driven improve mode on its own codebase, then rebuilds Docker and runs subsequent version benchmarks; resulting changed tool/prompt/coding policy becomes future subordinate S1 capability used by MainOrchestrator&#39;s current-work organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Long-term changes in measured coding benchmark performance and repeated feature failures, rather than current tool-loop transient errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model chooses which agent architecture/tool/behavior feature should be changed after comparing archived experience and review committee feedback, with real downstream implementation and retest. - Decision owner: Meta-improvement MainOrchestrator/CodingAgent and archive analysis model in the explicit self-improvement operating mode; version selection/testing is first-party deterministic support. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Benchmark registry, archive analyzer with utility/CI, selectbaseagent, independent model-driven ReviewCommittee, meta-improvement reasoning structure, agentchangelog.md, isolated next-agent directory. - Closure path: External benchmark/task outcomes → version-level archive → model diagnosis and novel future improvement option → separate committee critique → actual coding changes to next version → newly launched/evaluated agent iteration and updated archive. - Boundary reachability: Documented python runner.py default cycle executes benchmarks and then runmetaagentbenchmark, which launches baseagent improve mode. That mode integrates meta-improvement reasoning and committee. No external company service is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Future capability choice and proposal/refinement are model-authored, not merely a static benchmark-triggered template or heuristic version selector; first-party script closes deployment/evaluation. - Evidence: runner.py; baseagent/src/tools/reasoningstructures/metaimprovement.py; baseagent/src/agents/implementations/mainorchestrator.py; baseagent/src/tools/committeedesign.py; baseagent/src/agents/implementations/archiveexplorer.py - Basis: explicit / structural - Confidence: medium-high for model-driven improvement cycle, low for empirical net improvement - Caveats: A new iteration is produced even when benchmark gains are not established; selection uses observed statistics but does not prove monotonic improvement, safety or benchmark transfer. No production performance results inferred.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No evidenced legitimate ultimate-purpose/policy authority with identity-level deliberation and binding return across all future agent adaptations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Risk of benchmark Goodharting, security tradeoffs, resource spending and deciding which future changes are acceptable. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Humans configure objectives/budget, benchmark suite, model credentials and Docker trust. Committee can critique designs but cannot ratify ultimate identity or adjudicate mission change. - Decision owner: External experiment operator and developer-set prompt/policy boundaries; no dedicated legitimate S5 body. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ReviewCommittee &#39;taste maker&#39;, testing manager, fixed guards, benchmark score criterion, sandbox/budget limits. - Closure path: Committee reports recommendations and model may refine next feature; no identity-level issue/ultimate authority/legitimate binding policy return at S5 recursion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A sophisticated self-improvement S4 or design-review committee does not imply governing constitutional/organizational identity authority. - Evidence: baseagent/src/tools/committeedesign.py; runner.py; baseagent/src/agents/implementations/reviewcommitteemember.py; baseagent/src/config.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3efb20aba48801604f7585275a138d80bcb905ee/assessments/sica.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3efb20aba48801604f7585275a138d80bcb905ee\">3efb20a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T22:28:43+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Snowflake-Labs/cocoplus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CocoPlus</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Snowflake-Labs/cocoplus/tree/b252a8e7e09da6ca3178000d1fb5f48783dffc13\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b252a8e7e09da6ca3178000d1fb5f48783dffc13\">b252a8e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: specialist/persona agents autonomously perform the data-engineering/project transformations defined by the active CocoPlus goal/flow. - Disturbance / variety regulated: requirements, code/data artifacts, tool/Snowflake results, tests, implementation uncertainty and bounded project-state changes within each specialist remit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose local implementation/analysis/tool actions needed to complete the assigned stage/iteration outcome. - Decision owner: model-driven CocoPlus specialist/producer agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: personas, Snowflake Coco host, phase/stage contracts, model-tier floors, isolated worktrees, artifacts and tool/policy hooks. - Closure path: specialists produce artifacts/evidence in their bounded stage; outputs are persisted and passed to later stage/review/current-control paths, affecting subsequent project work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic gates/hook policy restrict actions but do not choose the specialist&#39;s substantive solution; the model-driven actor owns local operational judgment. - Evidence: README.md and CocoForge/CocoFlow documentation describe specialist agents executing build/test/review/project stages and autonomous long-running modes. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: regulate dependencies and destructive concurrent interference among specialist/producer S1 units while preserving useful parallelism. - Disturbance / variety regulated: dependency-invalid stage starts, concurrent producers touching overlapping file scopes, incomplete work contaminating another agent&#39;s context and oscillation between parallelism levels after failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which approved-plan work can run simultaneously versus must wait, and adapt stage parallelism from current dependency/failure conditions. - Decision owner: model-driven CocoHarvest/Team Lead coordination actor operating under the first-party flow contract. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dependency graph/gates, isolated worktrees, disjoint producer filescope, checkpoint artifacts, concurrency/retry budgets and deterministic dispatch guards. - Closure path: the coordination decision changes which stages/producers dispatch, which wait, and the scope they receive; dependency/checkpoint failures and disjoint-scope enforcement prevent unsafe execution until the coordination condition changes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: dependency/worktree mechanisms enforce safe boundaries, while the agentic harvest/lead path chooses the useful parallelization/coordination response within them. - Evidence: docs/features.html calls CocoHarvest the intelligence behind parallelism and describes dependency-aware waits/isolated worktrees/adaptive stage parallelism; AGENTS.md.template requires concurrent producer scopes to remain disjoint. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: plain phase sequencing is not credited; the positive witness is the concrete dependency/write-contamination disturbance with an agent-owned parallelism decision. - Distinct S1 units: two or more specialist/persona/producer agents executing separate CocoFlow/CocoForge/CocoFleet work. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping writes or dependency-invalid parallel stages can contaminate shared state, create conflicting outputs or cause downstream work to start from incomplete prerequisites. - Attenuating coordination relation: CocoHarvest/Team Lead chooses parallel versus waiting/dependency order; disjoint file scopes, isolated worktrees and checkpoint gates enforce that chosen separation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: only admitted stages/producers dispatch, blocked dependencies remain waiting, and failure/regression signals can narrow parallelism before later execution. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the mechanism is explicitly tied to preventing cross-agent contamination and dependency conflict, not merely assigning or ordering tasks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate current whole-run commitments, specialist dispatch, iteration/milestone progression, budgets, recovery and live intervention. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failing iterations, budget pressure, stalled stages, specialist output disagreement, quality-gate failure, current run-policy constraints and exceptions requiring developer intervention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: autonomous mode — choose iteration plan/dispatch/synthesis/next milestone response and current recovery within hard bounds; parent mode — clear human-gated stages, steer/stop/resume or resolve a dispute when current-control authority is deliberately reserved to the developer. - Decision owner: autonomous mode — model-driven Team Lead/run coordinator; parent mode — developer/operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: forge-state.json goal/iteration/phase/budget/team/history, milestone gates, run-policy snapshots, stall detection, retry budgets, AGENTSTOP/STEER, human-gate files and deterministic pre-tool policy hooks. - Closure path: autonomous lead decisions change specialist assignments, iteration state and next-stage progression; parent decisions clear/retain gates, resolve disputes or stop/steer the run, and the persisted state/policy governs subsequent agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Team Lead owns current orchestration within the established run boundary; hard hooks enforce constraints, while explicitly documented human gates/dispute/steering paths preserve a separate parent-owned mode rather than being attributed to the runtime. - Evidence: forge.skill.md assigns plan/dispatch/synthesis/milestone-gate decisions to Team Lead and persists whole-run state; AGENTS.md.template supplies AGENTSTOP/STEER, non-bypassable human gates and run-policy boundaries; docs/features.html supplies recovery/escalation/adaptive stage control. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: static policy enforcement by hooks is not credited as S3 ownership; the mapping depends on Team Lead discretion and explicit returned parent decisions. - Whole-system current view: current forge goal, iteration, phase, budget status, team, history, stage dependencies, evidence/critic status and run-policy snapshot. - Current-control decision scope: iteration plan, specialist dispatch, synthesis, milestone progression, current recovery/parallelism and developer-gated intervention.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge specialist/producer quality claims before milestone/signoff using a structurally separated critic path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: producer self-approval, rationalization, hidden quality regressions and acceptance of evidence that does not meet stated criteria. - Decisive decision or feedback right: issue PASS/REJECT quality judgment from the independent evidence presented to the critic and gate progression accordingly. - Decision owner: model-driven Quality Critic / read-only fleet critic. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: critic read-only role, lack of specialist reasoning traces, default-REJECT stance, producer/critic separation, required PASS signoff and milestone/refinement gates. - Closure path: critic verdict enters the milestone/flow state; rejection prevents acceptance/progression and drives another fix iteration/dispute, while PASS permits the governed continuation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the critic agent owns the quality judgment; read-only/separation/gating mechanisms preserve independence and enforce its consequences but do not produce the judgment. - Evidence: AGENTS.md.template states that the Quality Critic receives iteration outputs but not specialist reasoning traces, starts from REJECT and cannot be overruled by Team Lead; fleet mode separates producers from read-only critics and can require PASS; forge.skill.md places critic verdict in each milestone gate. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: routine tests/evidence gates are not the S3 witness; the mapping is specifically the independent critic path with complementary output-level access and non-overridable feedback. - Claim being audited: that specialist/producer iteration output meets the stated goal/quality/evidence criteria and is safe to accept for progression. - Ordinary reporting path: specialist/producer returns its output/artifacts into the Team Lead/flow iteration. - Complementary access path: separate Quality Critic/read-only critic receives iteration outputs/evidence without the specialist reasoning trace and evaluates them under its own criteria/default-REJECT stance. - Independence boundary: critic is separated from producer, read-only in fleet mode, lacks producer reasoning traces and cannot be overruled by Team Lead. - Who acts on findings: Team Lead/flow milestone control consumes PASS/REJECT; rejection forces another iteration, withheld signoff or dispute escalation before progression.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: inspect externally changed model capability after an upgrade, determine how that changes the value of existing governance/harness rules and develop evidence-backed adaptation options for future operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a newer model may make standing rules/checks newly redundant, advisory or still necessary, creating risk of stale harness constraints or unsafe premature deletion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge which recurring rule/check should remain load-bearing, degrade to advisory or be proposed as obsolete after model-capability change. - Decision owner: constructor path — CocoPlus supplies the S4-specific model-upgrade hygiene analysis, but the standard path does not autonomously close destructive rule changes; developer composition/authority is still required for returned adaptation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: $hygiene --model-upgrade, evidence collection across sessions/committed artifacts, retrospective/benchmark recommendations and prohibition on silent deletion. - Closure path: hygiene produces evidence-backed keep/advisory/obsolete recommendations intended to change recurring hooks/skills/standing context, but destructive adaptation is proposed rather than automatically applied; downstream developer action must complete the return into present capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an agent can perform the prospective analysis, yet CocoPlus intentionally withholds autonomous closure over the recurring harness rules; this is therefore a supplied S4 constructor path rather than A. - Evidence: cocohygiene/hygiene.skill.md explicitly defines a model-upgrade audit of governance rules/harness checks and classifies their future status while requiring evidence and proposing rather than silently applying deletions; AGENTS.md.template directs retrospective/hygiene/model benchmarking before changing recurring rules. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: generic retrospectives/learning are not credited as S4. The positive mapping is narrowly the external model-upgrade path; C reflects incomplete first-party adaptation closure. - External distinction: the capability/behavior of the upgraded external model relative to the assumptions under which current CocoPlus rules were created. - Future / prospective distinction: whether recurring governance/harness checks will remain necessary, become merely advisory or become obsolete for subsequent sessions/runs. - Adaptation option generated: retain the rule, downgrade its standing, replace/adjust it or propose removal when evidence shows it is obsolete. - Path back into current capability / S3: the recommendation targets recurring hooks/skills/standing context used by current runs, but developer action is still needed to apply the proposed structural change; this incomplete closure is why the state is C.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct runtime identity/ultimate-policy function is established at the reviewed project/run recursion beyond developer-authored policies, covenants and safety boundaries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: autonomy covenants, hard stops, run policies, safety rules and human approvals constrain operation, but the evidence does not show a legitimate S5 identity/ultimate-policy conversation with closure separate from those constraints/current-control gates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party S5 decision right is established. - Decision owner: none established for a qualifying S5 function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: covenant acceptance, run-policy snapshots, hard safety gates, human gates, policy engine, steering restrictions and persistent project configuration. - Closure path: these controls constrain subsequent operation, but no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate S5 judgment → returned organizational policy loop is established at the chosen recursion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither a static covenant/policy nor human approval over ordinary current work is sufficient to establish S5; no autonomous or parent S5 closure is inferred from enforcement. - Evidence: README.md, AGENTS.md.template, forge.skill.md and pre-tool-use.js were inspected for ultimate-policy/identity authority; the evidenced functions are current governance, safety and developer control. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e7e949d44dbe4f440ff6ff73d380893b661b9351/assessments/cocoplus.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e7e949d44dbe4f440ff6ff73d380893b661b9351\">e7e949d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T15:07:59+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Xtra-Computing/MegaAgent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MegaAgent</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-21\">2026-09-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Xtra-Computing/MegaAgent/tree/c2e45ad99d8166db82b8f8516d2bcd722ad8540a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c2e45ad99d8166db82b8f8516d2bcd722ad8540a\">c2e45ad &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously execute a bounded project contribution through a model-driven local task/tool loop and produce shared artifacts, tests, integration or other project outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task ambiguity, collaborator messages, current shared artifacts, execution/test results, local TODO/status, tool failures and changing subtask needs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose local tool actions, artifact changes, communication, testing/execution steps and whether to recruit subordinate capacity within assigned constraints. - Decision owner: each model-driven Agent operational unit. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-agent queue/thread, memory and relevant-history retrieval, generated role prompt, bounded model rounds, task/TODO files, first-party tools, shared workspace and external model transport. - Closure path: assigned/local task + collaborator/artifact state → agent model decision → tool/message/file action → returned execution/file/peer observation → subsequent model decision → local deliverable/result. - Boundary reachability: root main.py directly instantiates the generated CEO and workers through the first-party Agent class; recursive addagent uses the same runtime. No downstream agent loop has to be supplied to obtain model-driven operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic queue/tool/runtime code transports and enforces actions, but substantive local work, tool selection, content and follow-up are chosen by each running model agent from current evidence. - Evidence: main.py, agent.py, llm.py. - Basis: explicit + structural + observed bundled trace. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: not every generated process is automatically a separate viable recursion; S1 credits task-owning operational agent loops at the declared project level.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate dependency/readiness mismatch and conflicting concurrent mutations among distinct parallel agent S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: one unit acting before a required upstream artifact is available; two units observing/writing different versions of a shared artifact; stale overwrites and merge conflicts in the common project workspace. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide how to adjust local timing/work after collaborator readiness feedback and how to reconcile/retry a shared-artifact update after current content/hash or merge-conflict evidence is returned. - Decision owner: the affected model-driven operational agents, distributed across the collaborating S1 units; the Git lock/base-hash/merge machinery detects and enforces collision constraints but does not choose the revised content/work response. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: generated collaborator identities, asynchronous talk, per-agent TODO/status, shared file visibility, readfile current commit hash, writefile lock/base-hash requirement, Git commit/merge and conflict response. - Closure path: inter-agent dependency or shared-file collision risk → peer readiness message and/or version-aware write attempt → current readiness/content/hash/conflict evidence reaches affected agent → model chooses wait/retry/reconcile/change work → subsequent S1 behavior uses the coordinated state. - Boundary reachability: talk, shared-file read/write and Git conflict handling are standard tools generated for every root-runtime agent; the shipped Grace trace demonstrates the dependency-feedback path without downstream wiring. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a lock alone would be deterministic S2 support only. A is credited because concrete dependency/collision evidence is returned into a model-owned next decision: agents choose to wait, notify collaborators, reread or revise the content/work response. - Evidence: agent.py, llm.py, utils.py, logs/Grace.log. - Basis: structural + observed bundled trace. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic messaging/delegation is not the basis; the credited witnesses are concrete dependency timing and shared mutable-artifact collision. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently running task-owning agents such as implementation, integration and testing workers. - Inter-S1 disturbance: downstream work can begin before upstream deliverables exist, and parallel agents can act on stale/conflicting versions of shared files. - Attenuating coordination relation: peer readiness/blocker exchange plus hash-aware serialized Git mutation with current-state/conflict feedback. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the receiving model agent waits/resumes/reprioritizes or rereads/reconciles/retries against the returned shared state. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: each credited mechanism is tied to a concrete cross-unit instability—dependency readiness or concurrent shared-artifact mutation—and changes the next operational behavior that would otherwise interfere.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the project-wide current portfolio of commitments, priorities, readiness, defects and completion/rework on behalf of the whole organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete or blocked workers, changing dependency readiness, integration/testing findings, defects requiring reassignment, project-wide quiescence with unfinished work and apparent completion that may still fail whole-project requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose current task allocation/reallocation, testing priorities, corrective owner, additional agent capacity and whether the project should terminate or another work cycle should begin. - Decision owner: the model-driven CEO agent (Bob in the shipped default organization). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: worker talk reports, CEO subordinate map, shared project artifacts, per-agent TODO/status, global running/idle monitor, automatic wake-up of unfinished agents and final all-idle completion signal. - Closure path: current whole-project reports/artifacts/global completion state → CEO current-control judgment → talk/addagent/inspection/testing/reassignment decision → affected S1 unit changes work → new reports/artifacts/tests return into CEO control. - Boundary reachability: root main.py always creates the generated CEO, sends it the project-start allocation instruction, monitors every live agent/TODO, and returns apparent-completion state to that same CEO for terminate-versus-reassign judgment. The bundled Bob trace shows live priority and corrective routing in this standard path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the global monitor deterministically observes/wakes and supplies state, but it does not choose the organizational response. The CEO model chooses who should work on what next, which defect/priorities matter and whether to create another corrective cycle. - Evidence: main.py, agent.py, logs/Bob.log, logs/Grace.log. - Basis: structural + observed bundled trace. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: initial decomposition alone would not establish S3. The positive mapping rests on the later live worker/test feedback and CEO intervention/reassignment loop plus the whole-organization completion/rework closure. - Whole-system current view: worker progress/blocker/test messages, shared output artifacts and the root monitor&#39;s all-agent idle/TODO state provide the CEO with current project-level distinctions rather than a single-child result only. - Current-control decision scope: current ownership, priorities, testing focus, corrective reassignment, additional staffing and terminate-versus-rework decisions affecting multiple operational units and whole-project completion. - Current-control intervention: Bob redirects Dave after Grace identifies an AI defect, instructs Grace to continue/re-test and can recruit/message workers or restart work after the final completion check. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: talk/reassignment enters the relevant agent queue; corrected artifacts and new tester/integrator reports return through the same first-party organization. - Why this is S3-specific rather than decomposition / routing / static workflow: the credited witness occurs after initial decomposition and changes live commitments in response to current cross-project evidence and defects.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge ordinary implementation/integration claims by inspecting and executing produced artifacts, then return discrepancies into corrective project control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: latent functional defects or incomplete behavior that are not revealed by ordinary implementer progress/completion messages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine from direct artifact/runtime testing whether a produced behavior passes or exposes a concrete defect and publish that finding into project control. - Decision owner: the distinct model-driven testing agent in the generated organization (Grace in the shipped default run); CEO S3 owns the subsequent corrective allocation rather than the tester silently modifying the implementation claim. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tester role assignment, readfile, execpythonfile/interactive execution, test artifacts/reports, talk to CEO/integrator, shared workspace and CEO reassignment channel. - Closure path: implementer/integrator reports and produced artifacts → separate tester reads/executes/tests operational output → tester identifies discrepancy and reports evidence → CEO routes finding to responsible implementation S1 and orders follow-up/re-test → corrected operation is tested/reported again. - Boundary reachability: the standard root organization-generation path can and in the bundled default run does create a distinct tester; its runtime uses the same first-party tools/message channels and the committed logs show the full finding-to-correction handoff. No external evaluator is imported. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the testing judgment and finding are produced by a separate model agent using direct operational evidence. Deterministic execution/file tools expose reality but do not decide whether the observed behavior satisfies the claim. - Evidence: main.py, llm.py, logs/Grace.log, logs/Bob.log. - Basis: observed bundled trace + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic logs and the CEO&#39;s own routine project monitoring are not credited as S3. The positive witness is the separately assigned tester&#39;s direct artifact/runtime evidence and its corrective return. - Claim being audited: implementation/integration units report that delivered modules and assembled behavior are ready or functioning. - Ordinary reporting path: implementers/integrator send progress/readiness messages to collaborators/CEO and update shared deliverables/status. - Complementary access path: a distinct tester reads the actual produced files and executes targeted behavior/tests rather than accepting those status reports. - Independence boundary: Grace is separately generated/assigned as testing work, not the producer of the AI/integration implementation being challenged; her test observations are reported to the CEO rather than being generated by Dave&#39;s own production loop. - Who acts on findings: Grace reports the hard-AI four-in-a-row failure to Bob; Bob sends the finding to Dave for defensive-logic correction, directs Grace to continue testing, and the later organization requests re-testing/integration feedback. - Why this is S3-specific rather than ordinary review / QA / logging / evaluation: the evidence path materially bypasses ordinary implementer claims, directly probes runtime behavior, finds a contradiction and closes that finding into corrective operational work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no externally and prospectively oriented adaptation loop that changes future MegaAgent organizational capability is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the runtime reacts strongly to current task complexity, current shared artifacts and current test outcomes, but no evidenced function models changing external/future conditions for later organizational adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for selecting and promoting a future capability adaptation. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dynamic agent generation, recursive addagent, memory/history retrieval, current-task testing and task-specific reorganization. - Closure path: current task/complexity → current roster/subtask adaptation → current project execution; no prospective environmental distinction → future adaptation options → selected/promoted later capability → present S3 conversation is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents autonomously change current staffing and work in response to present operational variety, but Profile 0.2.3 explicitly separates current task planning/reaction from S4 future/environment intelligence. - Evidence: README.md, agent.py, config.py. - Basis: structural absence after prospective-adaptation review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: dynamically generating hundreds of agents demonstrates current variety amplification/scaling, not by itself future-oriented S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is established at the MegaAgent project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the user project goal, CEO name, model/provider choice, maximum rounds/subordinates and additional prompt constrain current operation, but no identity-level S3–S4 tension or ultimate-policy issue enters an authoritative runtime closure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5 publication. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: config.initialprompt, additionalprompt, user-edited project goal/provider settings, static limits and CEO role generation. - Closure path: no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned governance of subsequent operation path is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the CEO is an executive/control role for the current project, but executive naming and ordinary project completion authority are not S5. The user supplies initial purpose/configuration before operation; no parent runtime identity escalation path is shown. - Evidence: config.py, main.py, README.md. - Basis: structural absence after identity/policy review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream deployment could wrap MegaAgent in human/institutional governance, but that would be a different system boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2022cc46ef0c83cc6181ff994e9df4bb826f5d11/assessments/megaagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2022cc46ef0c83cc6181ff994e9df4bb826f5d11\">2022cc4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T21:45:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openags/auto-researcher\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenAGS</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openags/auto-researcher/tree/9bb9dfc6d5e66ad92e3652c8a2ed250d62f8c11f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9bb9dfc6d5e66ad92e3652c8a2ed250d62f8c11f\">9bb9dfc &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: specialist research agents perform the operational scientific work: literature review, proposal development, experiments, manuscript production, and peer review/revision artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: open-ended scientific questions, external literature, experiment implementation/execution, result interpretation, and manuscript construction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next specialist reasoning/tool/file action needed to produce each role&#39;s research outcome within its SOUL/skill contract. - Decision owner: each first-party builtin OpenAGS Agent executing the corresponding specialist folder/role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SOUL and skill loading, tool engine, memory/session/history, filesystem workspaces, upstream/downstream paths, LiteLLM transport, timeouts, and orchestrator lifecycle. - Closure path: observations/tool results return to the specialist Agent loop; the agent updates role artifacts/status and those outputs become available to downstream agents/coordinator. - Boundary reachability: the repository documents a complete first-party builtin Agent path (Agent.loop) used by the research layer; S1 therefore does not depend on Claude Code/Codex/Cursor/Gemini owning the operational loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the first-party agent engine closes model decision → tool execution → observation → next decision inside the supported builtin mode. - Evidence: builtin-agent execution path, folder-as-agent model, default specialist workflow. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: alternate CLI-agent backends are not used to justify S1 ownership; the credited mode is the self-contained builtin OpenAGS agent engine.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material S2-specific regulation of a demonstrated inter-S1 conflict/oscillation is established at the declared project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as S2. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: upstream/downstream file contracts, fixed first-pass sequence, TASKS/STATUS files, agent dispatch, and shared project artifacts organize work but do not by themselves establish an inter-S1 interference witness. - Closure path: workflow outputs feed downstream specialists, but that sequencing/dependency path is not sufficient for S2. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: named multi-agent roles and communication through files are generic organization/hand-off primitives unless tied to a concrete conflict or oscillation between S1 units and an attenuation loop. - Evidence: file-based agent communication, coordinator first-pass sequence. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: workflow dependency management may prevent some practical clashes, but the required S2-specific disturbance/attenuation witness is not established in the reviewed evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a whole-project current view and decide which specialist work starts, waits, reruns, or is complete. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete or failed stages, changing project status, downstream dependency readiness, review-identified weaknesses, and need to redirect current research effort. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next active specialist/task and, after the first pass, choose which research stages must rerun in response to current project evidence. - Decision owner: the root coordinator Agent in Auto Mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: specialist STATUS.md and TASKS.md, workflow/orchestrator process management, timeout/failure handling, fixed initial stage order, project files, and start/wait/all-complete action protocol. - Closure path: coordinator reads current statuses/review findings, emits startagent/wait/allcomplete/needshuman decisions and writes/assigns specialist tasks; the orchestrator launches or withholds subsequent work accordingly. - Boundary reachability: Auto Mode and the root coordinator are part of the default first-party project template and builtin workflow, not an external operator or development-only controller. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic orchestration executes start/stop/status mechanics, while the coordinator Agent is explicitly instructed to make pipeline decisions and decide what happens next from the whole project state. - Whole-system current view: all specialist status files, project-level memory/key decisions, current stage completion, and review outputs across the research project. - Current-control decision scope: assignment and ordering of current specialist work, whether to wait, whether the project is complete, and which stages to rerun after review. - Evidence: coordinator Auto Mode and role, workflow protocol. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: fixed first-pass sequencing is enforcement/support; S3 credit comes from the coordinator&#39;s evidenced discretionary current-control decisions, especially status-based dispatch and post-review rerun choice.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently review the manuscript/research claims using complementary access to manuscript, experiment results, literature notes, and external citation verification, then return actionable findings into project control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported claims, weak methodology/experiments, citation hallucination or misrepresentation, missing baselines/limitations, and paper-level weaknesses not caught by ordinary production. - Decisive decision or feedback right: make peer-review judgments, classify major/minor concerns, score review criteria, issue a verdict, and prescribe a revision roadmap. - Decision owner: the distinct review Agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dedicated review SOUL/folder, read access to manuscript/experiment/literature artifacts, web search for citation spot-checking, structured review format, and persisted review-report.md. - Closure path: review findings are written under review/reviews/; after the first pass the coordinator reads those weaknesses and decides which stages need to rerun, returning audit findings into changed subsequent operation. - Boundary reachability: the reviewer is a default specialist in the standard project template and a standard first-pass stage; its report is explicitly part of the coordinator&#39;s iteration mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer makes substantive adversarial/audit judgments rather than merely applying a deterministic syntax gate; the coordinator, not the reviewer, owns corrective project-control decisions. - Claim being audited: the manuscript&#39;s scientific claims, citations, experimental support, logic, completeness, and reproducibility. - Ordinary reporting path: manuscript output and specialist status/results flow normally into the project filesystem and coordinator. - Complementary access path: the reviewer reads the full manuscript plus experiment report plus literature review and independently web-searches sampled citations; it is instructed to adversarially probe alternative explanations/failure conditions/missing experiments. - Independence boundary: a separate review role/folder with its own SOUL and artifact is outside the manuscript-producing agent&#39;s ordinary production loop, while retaining direct access to underlying experiment/literature evidence. - Who acts on findings: the root coordinator reads review weaknesses and selects which stages to rerun; affected specialists then perform the corrective work. - Evidence: reviewer SOUL, coordinator iteration mode. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model/provider independence is not claimed; organizational independence comes from the distinct review role, separate artifact, complementary evidence surfaces, and explicit findings-to-control return path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: sense the external scientific landscape through the literature specialist and turn those distinctions into future-facing research proposals/experiment direction consumed by later S1 work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: risk of pursuing already-solved, poorly grounded, or scientifically irrelevant directions and missing externally established methods/results that should shape the project. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select and synthesize externally retrieved papers/field distinctions that should inform the project proposal and later research program. - Decision owner: the literature Agent, with downstream proposal/experiment agents autonomously using the returned research intelligence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: paper-search skills/web access, literature notes/memory, fixed upstream paths, shared project filesystem, and coordinator sequencing. - Closure path: literature agent searches/reads external work and writes literature notes; the proposal agent explicitly reads ../literature/notes/ and memory.md; experiment/manuscript stages consume the proposal/literature outputs, changing subsequent project work. - Boundary reachability: literature is a default first-party specialist stage in the project template, and its artifacts are wired as upstream inputs to subsequent specialists in every supported runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the first-party literature Agent decides what external evidence is relevant and synthesizes it; file plumbing only returns that intelligence to current capability. - External distinction: current prior papers, methods, results, and gaps retrieved from outside the project. - Future / prospective distinction: which field gaps/constraints should shape the proposal and experiments the project has not yet executed. - Adaptation option generated: literature-grounded research/proposal directions and constraints for experiment design. - Path back into current capability / S3: persisted literature notes become explicit upstream inputs to proposal and later experiment/manuscript work under coordinator sequencing. - Evidence: folder/agent upstream-downstream architecture, research workflow skill. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: project memory and peer review alone are not used to justify S4; the credit rests on external scientific sensing and a closed return path into future project work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the research-project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at S5 scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SOUL role definitions, user project brief, templates, skills, provider configuration, and needshuman escalation constrain operation but do not themselves establish ultimate-policy governance. - Closure path: no evidenced identity/policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned operation loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coordinator manages current work but is not shown redefining the project&#39;s ultimate identity/policy; generic human input/escalation is not automatically parent-governed S5. - Evidence: coordinator SOUL, architecture. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user can direct a project and the coordinator can request human input, but neither fact alone establishes S5 without a genuine ultimate-policy issue and authoritative return path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df/assessments/openags.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df\">c9da283 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-13T20:00:45+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/PawanOsman/OpenCursor\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenCursor</a><small>2022</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-13\">2026-10-13</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/PawanOsman/OpenCursor/tree/b4528f95ca5ab4cddf22bef3e19431fad00feb01\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b4528f95ca5ab4cddf22bef3e19431fad00feb01\">b4528f9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: directly interpret, inspect, edit and verify software against a human coding task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code and runtime behavior, incomplete edits, tool errors and feedback from repository/test inspection. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose file reads, edits, tool calls, retries, delegated S1 work and final response based on live evidence. - Decision owner: the model-driven coding agent in Agent mode and coding-worker subagents in supported Project/Multitask modes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: VS Code tool bridge, file and shell effect adapters, permission/approval policies, session persistence and job display. - Closure path: agent choice → tool execution/permission result → actual file/shell observation → subsequent model decision or answer. - Boundary reachability: the packaged extension directly wires its agent loop and repository tools, no custom embedding host or optional external agent workflow is necessary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the acting model leaves tool wrappers but no task-driven selection/repair; human approves permitted effects but does not choose every next coding action. - Evidence: README.md; loop.ts; index.ts; files.ts; approvalPolicy.ts - Basis: explicit and structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate shared-workspace change collisions and conflicting edits between independently acting delegated code-writing workers in the shipped coordinator/team modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: at least two concurrently active coding children may attempt incompatible modifications or overwrite/revert another worker&#39;s output in their shared checkout. - Decisive decision or feedback right: lead model selects nonoverlapping assignments, sends corrective messages when a child flags conflict, and revises affected children rather than blindly accepting conflicting edits. - Decision owner: the lead agent/model in opt-in Project/Multitask configurations; child agents report overlaps; deterministic mailbox and a concurrency cap only execute the decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task schema&#39;s &#39;assign separate files&#39; contract, built-in team protocol&#39;s non-overwrite/flag instruction, stable child IDs, collaborator status, message/follow-up controls. - Closure path: child conflict signal or current roster/result → lead&#39;s scope/plan correction → SendAgentMessage/FollowupAgent or new separate assignment → children&#39;s subsequent work; separation also happens prospectively when lead initially assigns distinct files. - Boundary reachability: built-in Project/Multitask prompt paths and child/messaging tools are wired in the standard extension, not derived from a user-created orchestration script. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task-scope choice and conflict response are discretionary lead-model decisions; the active-child cap and status store do not own that interference-specific decision. - Evidence: loop.ts; schemas.ts; teams.ts; backgroundTasks.ts; collaborationState.ts - Basis: explicit mode instructions and structural code-wired feedback - Confidence: medium; a supported decision path, not proof that every conflict is detected or prevented - Caveats: children usually share a workspace; conversation worktrees are optional isolation and not automatic per-child reservations. Ordinary Agent mode does not establish this coordinated path on every turn. - Distinct S1 units: concurrent model/tool-running coding children started via the Task tool, each with its own history, task and executable edit tools. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overwriting/reverting another child&#39;s files or incompatible edits in the shared workspace, explicitly addressed by the shipped Team protocol and Task tool. - Attenuating coordination relation: lead chooses separate file assignments; built-in team members must adapt/flag cross-member conflicts, while root can steer/interrupt/reassign them. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: child reports and roster responses enter the lead turn; message/follow-up deliver revised instructions to named still-running or resumed children. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the shipped instructions target a concrete shared-file interference, not only transporting tasks; lead feedback can alter worker scopes after conflict.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the set of active delegated commitments across current Project/Multitask coding operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple live coding children compete for bounded concurrent slots, priorities and dependencies while task results and interruptions change the feasible current plan. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the complete conversation collaborator roster/status and choose which current worker gets each unit, is steered, followed up or interrupted; revise running commitments in response to results. - Decision owner: the opt-in Project/Multitask lead model; not the fixed max-four gate, job registry, event stream or human authorization engine. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ListAgents and stable IDs, activeAgents map, child status/result feedback, SendAgentMessage/FollowupAgent/InterruptAgent, root&#39;s intentionally disabled editing tools. - Closure path: whole-session roster/task reports → lead&#39;s portfolio-level assignment/intervention decision → launch/message/interrupt and changed child work → updated statuses/results. - Boundary reachability: the standard Project and Multitask modes explicitly put an LLM lead over all delegated coding tasks with model-facing operational management tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the lead model leaves a cap and worker status API but not a discretionary choice of how to reallocate current work; the supported lead modes are wider than one routine child tool call. - Evidence: loop.ts; schemas.ts; collaborationState.ts; backgroundTasks.ts; taskState.ts - Basis: structural and explicit first-party coordinator-mode instructions - Confidence: medium; session-scoped, not organizational or multi-deployment management - Caveats: not attributed to ordinary single-worker Agent mode or separate VS Code sessions. Does not mean that all child steps are autonomously centralized. - Whole-system current view: ListAgents exposes all retained conversation collaborators, live status/result and queued-message counts; background job registry tracks running/completed work. - Current-control decision scope: lead-mode model decides present unit assignment, bounded concurrency slot utilization, correction/interrupt, follow-up and completion of delegated commitments.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently inspect the implementing agent&#39;s code claims using a separate read-only code-reviewer context and raw repository/diff evidence, then return findings for control of repairs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an implementer&#39;s self-report or ordinary integrated tool-status check may omit real defects, concurrency problems or unsafe edits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separate reviewer agent evaluates the actual diff and source context for defects; the implementing parent receives and decides follow-up repair or rejection. - Decision owner: the delegated reviewer model for the complementary judgment, acting only when commissioned, and the lead/implementer model for the downstream corrective choice. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in read-only code-reviewer and team-code-reviewer roles, child independent history, file-reading/search tools, returned report and parent Task result. - Closure path: parent requests review → separate child reads current repository/diff and forms defect-first findings → report returns to parent history → implementing lead can fix, stop or ask for further check. - Boundary reachability: built-in code-reviewer Task type and built-in team-code-reviewer are available in the shipped extension and run through first-party child-model/tool execution; no user-authored reviewer is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer performs an independent direct-evidence judgment; parent runtime reports/forwards but does not supply the reviewer&#39;s verdict. Ordinary VerificationLedger is excluded from S3 credit. - Evidence: teams.ts; schemas.ts; loop.ts; files.ts; verification.ts - Basis: explicit built-in independent reviewer instructions and source-wired child execution - Confidence: medium-high for opt-in separate-context code review, not for default every-turn checks - Caveats: review and corrective response are conditional on the model/parent invoking the supported reviewer; no claim of Organization S3 governance or fully independent remote operator. - Claim being audited: whether implementing-agent code modifications are correct, safe and maintainable. - Ordinary reporting path: implementer tool outputs, final answer and VerificationLedger test-status summary. - Complementary access path: read-only separately instantiated reviewer model with direct code/diff/repository access and explicit defect-first prompt. - Independence boundary: different child model context/history/role and direct artifacts rather than merely parsing the implementer&#39;s natural-language self-report. - Who acts on findings: calling lead/implementer receives findings through Task result and can select corrective work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate external-and-prospective organizational capability-adaptation loop is established in the distributed extension. - Disturbance / variety regulated: external documentation, model/tool changes and potential future feature requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped S4-specific adaptive decision/feedback owner identified. - Decision owner: model for present coding and documentation retrieval; user or extension settings for selecting models/skills; neither closes future adaptation of the harness organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: docs discovery/indexing, semantic repository retrieval, reusable personas/instructions, task memory and provider choice. - Closure path: information returns to task context, but no first-party future capability strategy is selected and returned to ongoing operational capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: document discovery and planning for current coding work is not a prospective S4 organizational judgment. - Evidence: docsPlanner.ts; loop.ts; README.md; taskState.ts - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy constitutional decision path with binding return is supplied for the coding-session organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ultimate purpose or policy conflicts as distinct from one tool&#39;s allowed/denied action. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party S5 decisive authority; ordinary permission review authorizes individual actions. - Decision owner: user/host settings and project operators outside the assessed system-in-focus; no evidenced internal/parent constitutional decision chain. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: approval policy, read-only modes, tool restrictions, workspace instructions and team role prompts. - Closure path: action approvals return allow/deny to ordinary task execution; no identity-level matter → parent ultimate decision → changed governing operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: tool policy is a safety constraint, not the identity-policy function; an operator&#39;s wider development organization is not imported into this one-session boundary. - Evidence: approvalPolicy.ts; loop.ts; schemas.ts; README.md; teams.ts - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/37c1aace4e11d50dbdda13b256534152d0169eea/assessments/opencursor.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 37c1aace4e11d50dbdda13b256534152d0169eea\">37c1aac &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-08T15:26:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dhoelzer/AgentOrchestrator\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Orchestrator Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-08\">2026-11-08</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dhoelzer/AgentOrchestrator/tree/33fce2cc0d0b3dd4dcd0e7267391b45a2d4edded\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 33fce2cc0d0b3dd4dcd0e7267391b45a2d4edded\">33fce2c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: specialists perform model-chosen work on project workspace files, commands and tracked deliverables; the root orchestrator is also a model-operated agent, but its regulatory decisions are credited under S2/S3 rather than double-counted as production operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concrete implementation/test/file problems and tool results encountered while completing assigned cards. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the specialist model chooses tool actions and reports completion/partial/blocking evidence inside its bounded assignment. - Decision owner: model-backed specialist agent, through OpenHands SDK conversation and first-party tool wiring. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: terminal/file-editor/task-tracker SDK tools, project sandbox, concurrency/turn ceilings, message and event ledger. - Closure path: the specialist changes real project files and returns results to its conversation/parent; the board captures execution/review state, which triggers further activity. - Boundary reachability: the shipped root main.py constructs the runtime and orchestrator; RunAgentExecutor calls dispatch, which passes tools to AgentRuntime.run and records subsequent outcomes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: action selection resides with the model-backed specialist; deterministic scheduler and SDK executor transport/enforce choices but do not pick the coding actions themselves. - Evidence: worker and orchestrator tool registry, runtime dispatch, SDK run. - Basis: structural source, supported startup and mocked tool/board tests. - Confidence: high for an installed agent-action path; no credentialed external-provider success run is claimed. - Caveats: OpenHands SDK and external LLM are required; their internal control planes are outside the first-party assessment boundary.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: keep independently operating specialist tasks from simultaneously interfering on shared project files and blocked dependencies. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two otherwise parallel workers touching the same file or a downstream worker starting against unfinished upstream output. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the root model decides task separation/dependency edges and can re-plan task relationships from each fresh board/attention briefing; the scheduler subsequently enforces the chosen edges mechanically. - Decision owner: root orchestrator agent for the conflict-prevention plan; deterministic scheduler for execution of that model-authored plan. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dependson task fields, sequential readiness, project-level slot cap, task statuses, generated briefings and tool-mediated updates. - Closure path: orchestrator chooses dependencies for overlapping work; scheduler refuses to start a dependent task until all predecessor cards are done; completion produces an unblock and new agent dispatch, while blocker/rework feedback allows the next orchestrator cycle to change the plan. - Boundary reachability: the root system prompt explicitly commands dependson when parallel tasks would touch the same files, CreateTaskExecutor stores model-chosen edges, and the shipping scheduler checks them on every sweep. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: conflict-sensitive choice of edges is a model tool call, not an authored static DAG. The runtime&#39;s bare dependency check is not independently S2=A, and evidence does not establish automatic real-time file-lock conflict detection. - Distinct S1 units: separately created project specialists, with each one owning a model/file workspace execution stretch. - Inter-S1 disturbance: named shared-file collision between parallel task workers, together with downstream use of unfinished upstream files. - Attenuating coordination relation: model-authored dependson serializes the interfering work while leaving unrelated cards eligible for concurrent execution. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: predecessor done releases a card to ready, next scheduler dispatch injects task/dependency notes and prior review feedback; root receives current board statuses for re-planning. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the supplied orchestrator prompt ties dependency choice to a particular inter-worker file collision, and the dependency gate actively controls conflicting worker start; generic task order alone would not support this claim. - Evidence: root planning rule, task edges/edit, dependency scheduler, dependency test. - Basis: explicit operating prompt, source-enforced dependency return, local stub tests. - Confidence: medium: the intended shared-file interference is explicit, but live model identification of correct dependency edges was not observed. - Caveats: no actual file-overlap detector or automatic cross-task lock. A model that omits needed edges can still cause collision; mere slot caps are not credited as the agent-owned S2 decision.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a whole-project current board and regulate allocations, staffing, blocked recovery, review and delivery decisions across worker cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unstaffed work, spent budgets, project stalls, failed models, review/rework queues and imbalanced worker/endpoint capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the root model chooses task decomposition, worker assignment, budget extension versus reconfiguration/splitting, current plan edits, reviewer dispatch and explicit delivery signal from new board feedback. - Decision owner: the continuously reused root orchestrator model/agent; human edits, pause and config controls impose constraints and may override but do not constitute the credited autonomous path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persisted Store board, detect() attention, cyclebriefing, createtask, assigntask, extendagent, reconfigureagent, retireagent, config reload, slot gate and deterministic Scheduler.sweep. - Closure path: issue/change/block appears in Store; the next root cycle receives a cross-card and capacity briefing, issues a chosen mutation through board tools, and tasks are dispatched/reviewed/blocked accordingly; later results return to that same Store and next-cycle decisions. - Boundary reachability: main.py launches the never-ending loop; runcycle checks live attention, runprojectcycle uses root SDK model and passes the root board tools, and scheduler runs both before and independently of the root cycle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: staffing and recovery choices disappear when the root model is removed. Automatic capacity accounting and status transition are supporting controller mechanics, not the entire S3 claim. - Whole-system current view: per-project board, dependencies, agents, blockers, reviewer status, cost/capacity and user messages in fresh root briefings, across the managed specialist S1 cells. - Current-control decision scope: project portfolio task and worker commitments, resourcing, rework direction and delivery timing; no claim that the model autonomously rewrites system-level policy/LLM infrastructure. - Evidence: attention/blocked detection, cycle and project control, orchestrator authority tools, root discipline. - Basis: shipped loop, named board decisions and tests of blocking/dispatch. - Confidence: high for architectural agent-owned project regulation. - Caveats: global cross-project cycle is sequential per project; no separate autonomous cross-domain S3 beyond the installed project portfolio is inferred.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: a distinct reviewer agent independently inspects actual files/testable results against task acceptance criteria and returns a corrective verdict on worker output. - Disturbance / variety regulated: erroneous implementation or unsupported worker claims surviving routine completion reporting. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in a configured reviewer mode, a separate model reviewer decides approved versus changesrequested after reading files; RecordReviewExecutor translates that judgment into done or rework and the root re-dispatches on corrective feedback. - Decision owner: a separate model reviewer when explicitly equipped with the recordreview tool; the orchestrator remains parent control of staffing/response. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: distinct reviewer task link, reviewer assignment prompt, read-file/terminal SDK tools, reconfigureagent, review rounds, feedback persistence and rework board transitions. - Closure path: worker finishes to review; parent creates/attaches reviewer; reviewer opens independent file read, checks criteria and records verdict; failing work moves to rework with review feedback in the next worker run. - Boundary reachability: default shipping tool registry permits a root to reconfigure a newly created reviewer with recordreview, then dispatch runagent(review=true); the tool writes verdict and rework state. However CreateSubAgentExecutor validates action.tools but fails to assign it to AgentSpec.toolsoverride, while the default worker tool set omits recordreview. The advertised one-step reviewer creation does not itself close a direct verdict. This is a composable, not reliably default mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: distinct reviewer model rather than creator&#39;s same tool state can inspect actual files and choose a verdict; it needs explicit tool wiring, and default root self-inspection alone is not counted as independent S3. - Claim being audited: a worker&#39;s claim that named project deliverables satisfy task-specific acceptance criteria. - Ordinary reporting path: worker&#39;s reporttoparent and normal task completion/summary, which are not themselves independent proof. - Complementary access path: reviewer readfile/terminal reads workspace artifacts and may run tests independently from worker&#39;s self-report. - Independence boundary: different child agent/conversation and reviewer pointer from worker&#39;s assignedagentid; both share first-party project files, so no stronger sandbox/process isolation is inferred. - Who acts on findings: reviewer verdict switches board done/rework, with orchestrator/scheduler returning failure feedback to the next worker stretch. - Evidence: reviewer prompts, creation tool omission, reconfigure tool/registry, review submission, reviewer scheduler. - Basis: source-level executable mode and mocked board tests, no live reviewer provider run. - Confidence: medium for the explicitly equipped mode; low for author claims of out-of-box automatic reviewer finalization. - Caveats: reviewer creation&#39;s tools argument is presently a no-op at the pin, and reviewerprompt is defined but not automatically selected by CreateSubAgentExecutor. Default prompts alone must not be treated as verified independent adjudication.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate agent-owned environmental scanning, future-option generation and implemented organizational adaptation loop was identified in the installed distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential future tool/platform/model change would require a prospective response, but the observed scheduler chiefly handles current throughput and present user cards. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none evidenced for autonomous outside/then strategy; endpoint selection and automatic timeouts are current deterministic transport tuning. - Decision owner: user changes configured LLM servers and task goals; root agent replans current work but is explicitly told not to change harness configuration itself. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: config reload, model endpoint router health signals, new user-input attention, arbitrary specialist prompts. - Closure path: no first-party agent-origin external trend/forecast option is chosen and carried into a durable future capability or strategy change. A user-set configuration may reload but is not such a loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: endpoint tuning and iterative project replanning are S1/S3 current-control support, not evidence of an independently closed S4 function. - Evidence: config reload, do not reconfigure rule, current-attention routing. - Basis: shipped model action inventory, operating prompt and selected state/endpoint paths. - Confidence: medium-high within specified first-party runtime modes. - Caveats: this is not a claim that human maintainers cannot plan future versions outside the harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate first-party ultimate identity/constitutional policy authority is exposed as a separately closed S5 decision process. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing portfolio purposes or permanent policy contradictions would require legitimate identity-level arbitration, but observed choices are task/agent instructions and runtime configuration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none evidenced beyond task-level human direction and root-controlled specialist prompt/mission changes. - Decision owner: static installed root ORCHESTRATORSYSTEMPROMPT and the human software owner define core identity; the model can create/reconfigure subagent instructions but not set the harness&#39;s own ultimate policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: frozen system prompt, config fields, project scope, human UI pause/manual delivery, per-agent systemprompt overrides. - Closure path: no first-party policy dispute adjudicator with changed identity fed back into the root runtime; changes to child missions remain subordinate S3 control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a root&#39;s ability to author a new worker prompt is delegation/operational policy, not legitimate authority to rewrite its own constitutional identity; static operator settings do not imply a distinct parent S5 mode. - Evidence: fixed root identity, root spec creation, child prompts/reconfiguration. - Basis: first-party identity/prompt tooling search and source inspection. - Confidence: medium-high for absence in the supported runtime boundary. - Caveats: external repository governance by maintainers is not part of the installed agent application assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0a07f100b820ab800e685f36a8047b6cfc54d18d/assessments/or/orchestrator-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0a07f100b820ab800e685f36a8047b6cfc54d18d\">0a07f10 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T22:05:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/skrvidz/agentic-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agentic Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/skrvidz/agentic-harness/tree/c67b6cec088d8f0054d9626adad6a22ea87fdbd6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c67b6cec088d8f0054d9626adad6a22ea87fdbd6\">c67b6ce &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-led project implementation via Claude or Codex engineer turns, including tool/repository feedback and task revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing local source and tests, uncertain implementation requirements, reviewer defects, failed commands and subsequent model observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose code edits, test/inspection commands, interpretation of feedback and implementation strategy inside the supplied objective/current task. - Decision owner: Spawned engineer model CLI as explicit task-operating actor, bounded by Harness and its model/tool permissions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ClaudeRunner/CodexEngineerRunner, persistent state, per-run worktrees, test evidence, max-cycles and reviewer feedback file. - Closure path: Human objective → engineer model turn and code edits → terminal/tool feedback → engine captures outcome → separate review → revised task fed into next engineer turn or final result. - Boundary reachability: agent-harness run and workspace Run create a real engineer subprocess, while --dry-run is explicitly separate and not credited for autonomous operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Engineer model chooses substantive implementation; removing it leaves only runner/orchestration and scripted test fixtures. - Evidence: CLI invocation, subprocess runners, engineer turn. - Basis: structural first-party executable path. - Confidence: High for decision-loop wiring, medium for actual provider behavior without an authenticated run. - Caveats: The harness does not implement provider transport; installed authenticated CLI models are required outside dry-run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No qualifying S2 loop that detects operational-unit interaction-induced disturbance and returns attenuation into independent peer behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel plan tickets may conflict during Git integration, or dependencies may block scheduling; these are prevented/flagged by fixed isolation/dependency checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Static scheduler selects ready DAG nodes; merge conflicts fail/pause rather than a distinct S2 coordinator negotiating or adapting peer commitments. - Decision owner: Model planner authored the initial task graph; deterministic PlanHarness enforces it with fixed width, worktrees and merge checks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Validated dependson, ready[:width], bounded asyncio.gather, isolated TicketWorkspace and fail-on-conflict integration. - Closure path: Plan → independent ticket loops → deterministic merge or pause; no observed conflict signal → inter-S1 attenuation decision → peer behavior correction path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A dependency graph and isolation are generic task sequencing. Model planning is not proven to monitor competing peers and damp induced interference during current execution. - Evidence: DAG scheduler, worktrees/merge. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Model could have chosen safer dependencies ex ante, but a hypothetical conflict-attenuation behavior is not an evidenced first-party closed S2 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Current-goal control of implementation commitments and task direction based on whole-run state and independent evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Code that does not satisfy goal/acceptance tests, unresolved scope decisions, an incomplete increment, and uncertainty whether a wider ticket plan achieved the objective. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reviewer decides APPROVED with a next task, REVISE with required changes, DONE or NEEDSHUMAN; the plan-level evaluator judges the integrated project against the whole original objective and may pause delivery. - Decision owner: Dedicated reviewer model for the single-run organization; in plan mode, dedicated planner/final-evaluator model for whole-ticket commitment judgment. Harness applies decisions to future task/current status. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Typed review schema, recorded run state/criteria, feedback file, nextactor transition, bounded cycle budget, SQLite event artifacts and final-plan evaluation. - Closure path: Current project evidence + engineer work → reviewer compares objective/criteria → chooses revision/next increment/completion/human gate → engine adjusts the next S1 task or stops. In DAG mode, integrated ticket results and source evidence → planner&#39;s final verdict → whole-goal completion or pause. - Whole-system current view: reviewerprompt receives the complete current objective/criteria and source-backed diff/status/test evidence; plannerfinalprompt additionally receives every ticket outcome, full plan and integrated-repo evidence. - Current-control decision scope: Approve a sound increment and redirect the next task, require corrective rework, declare current run complete or require human decision; final plan acceptance can be withheld. This is discretionary present-task commitment control, not a deterministic max-cycle or width guard. - Boundary reachability: Every default engineer turn is followed by reviewer control in Harness.runasync; plan mode invokes final model evaluation after all ticket integrations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model selects the quality/commitment verdict and next operational instruction from evidence; the Python engine merely branches on the verdict to update S1 commitments. Parent human involvement is a pause/answer gate, not claimed as a separate S3(P) mode. - Evidence: review verdict and actions, current-state prompts, final planner control. - Basis: explicit + structural first-party model choice and return path. - Confidence: Medium-high; model verdict rights are explicit though semantic quality is unobserved in live providers. - Caveats: No independent fleet budget arbitration claimed; scoped to goal commitment/current work in one configured harness.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Separate evidence-facing review of operational completion claims and test outcomes before continued implementation or acceptance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Engineer can confidently report a completed change that fails acceptance criteria, hides untracked files or contains regressions not captured in its summary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Read-only reviewer supplies structured findings and REVISE/DONE/APPROVED verdict from direct repository/test evidence; engine records it and routes corrections. - Decision owner: Distinct Codex reviewer CLI session (fresh read-only sandbox), separate from edit-capable engineer; the independent final planner evaluates integrated evidence in DAG mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: collectevidence runs real Git diff/status/untracked listing and optional project test command; review prompt calls engineer text untrusted; Codex --sandbox read-only and JSON schema; persisted audit raw artifacts. - Closure path: Engineer&#39;s report → independent evidence capture plus reviewer primary-source inspection → verdict and cited findings → reviewerfeedback.md/currenttask → next engineer turn, or blocked human decision/completion. - Claim being audited: Work delivered in the active turn meets the stated objective and acceptance criteria and can be accepted as done or as a sound increment. - Ordinary reporting path: The engineer summary and external CLI stream, persisted as pendingengineersummary and transcript artifacts. - Complementary access path: Git diff/stat/status and untracked file listing from the actual working tree, configured test-command output and reviewer direct read-only access to the code, plus independent integrated working-tree evidence for final plan review. - Independence boundary: Separate subprocess agent and session; fresh Codex reviewers start under read-only sandbox, cannot directly modify engineer&#39;s work. Captured evidence is provided by harness code, not inferred from engineer text; resumed sessions retain prior policy. - Who acts on findings: Harness.applyreview converts REVISE into the next engineer assignment; NEEDSHUMAN forces a human question; DONE gates completion. Planner can withhold whole-plan completion. - Boundary reachability: Review is mandatory after each successful engineer turn in default Harness, with nextactor=reviewer durable even when the cycle limit is exhausted. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Independent evidence-backed reviewer judgments are not the same as generic logs or unit tests; they determine whether the operation is revised, continued or completed. - Evidence: evidence collection, read-only reviewer runner, review feedback return. - Basis: explicit + structural independent reviewer path. - Confidence: High for separation, evidence path and corrective wiring; no provider-backed audit of actual generated changes performed here. - Caveats: Missing configured test command is marked unverified; evidence bounded/truncated to 40k chars, and a read-only CLI sandbox still relies on vendor client enforcement outside the first-party product.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective organizational adaptation that changes future harness capabilities in response to an external environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future implementation milestones are decomposed from the present task; no separate environmental foresight is continuously collected to change agent organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model planner defines the current goal&#39;s ticket list, but cannot alter the harness&#39;s future capability, governance or strategy; user changes model/settings manually. - Decision owner: Planner for near-term task plan and human for future system configuration; no S4 actor established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DAG plan/review, stored transcripts and model selection configuration. - Closure path: Goal → model-authored near-term tickets → implementation; no future environment scan → adaptation option → selection → return to future operating capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Time-ordered ticket planning is present operational decomposition, not strategic outside-and-then environmental intelligence. - Evidence: planner prompt, DAG execution. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: An outside organization could adopt findings to redesign its tooling, but that return is not wired in the assessed package.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No recurring ultimate organizational identity and policy arbitration loop within the product. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe edits, unsupported decisions and risky plan changes are bounded by configured CLI sandbox/limits and human pause. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human sets objective and decides blocked product/scope questions; no first-party actor adjudicates enduring identity or constitutional policy. - Decision owner: Human user for objective and particular decision; editor/CLI sandbox for fixed access bounds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Human decision callback/CLI, max cycles, explicit noninteractive pause, read-only reviewer setting and worktree constraints. - Closure path: Risk/product question → human decision → next engineer task, or configured hard limit → pause; neither constitutes policy identity dispute → ultimate arbitration → binding organizational-policy change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Operational question/approval and fixed sandbox policy are not S5 policy-authority decision rights. - Evidence: human decision handling, README safety boundary. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: User&#39;s employer or external host may retain ultimate policy authority outside this local harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/717a8b4606aa4ea034e6108cf2200e696f90de56/assessments/ag/agentic-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 717a8b4606aa4ea034e6108cf2200e696f90de56\">717a8b4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T22:25:03+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Sinkear/agentwork\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">agentwork</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Sinkear/agentwork/tree/3aecbef55ac694d64cb77403092aa850279b60a8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3aecbef55ac694d64cb77403092aa850279b60a8\">3aecbef &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: CLI-model execution of assigned goals and delegated sub-goal code/work tasks, with context and tool-result feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing repo contents, requirements, test failures, independent reviewer verdicts, integration-base shifts, consulted advice and human rejection reasons. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose substantive work/edits/tool calls and responses in owner/sub-goal runs; local assignee chooses how to repair a rejected or conflicting revision. - Decision owner: External connected CLI model running under first-party agentwork role/identity token as an explicit operational actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Daemon/machine dispatcher, per-run worktree, assigned agent persona, ACP/JSONL/JSON-RPC transport, role-scoped CLI, timeouts/watchdogs, stateful resume and Git worktree snapshot. - Closure path: Human goal → owner run/model plans/directs → assignee model executes local project changes and reports → run terminal/verification signals → feedback/wake/context into successor agent turns → revised task or completion. - Boundary reachability: Documented agentwork connect launches configured agent CLI runtimes on machine dispatch, and the first-party service creates owner/sub-goal runs with explicit role context. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The models decide which sub-goals to create, tools to invoke and code changes to make; deterministic state reconciliation and permission checks are support, not task reasoning. - Evidence: README quick start, run prompt assembly, sub-goal creation. - Basis: explicit + structural first-party operational integration. - Confidence: High for implemented role/feedback reachability, medium for live external-model behavior. - Caveats: No authenticated target-domain agent process was launched in this review; internal behaviors of ACP/CLI backends are not imported.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Detect and attenuate conflicts between independently produced sub-goal changes, directing the responsible assignee to rebase/rework rather than silently integrating an incompatible revision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different S1 sub-goal units edit against a shared goal branch and create incompatible integration bases or merged changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A failed owner integration marks a specific Change conflicting, invalidates its prior verified state and enqueues the same assignee on the latest goal base; owner later chooses integration of a new ready revision. - Decision owner: First-party constructor-owned service transition and event/goal coordinator establish the S2-specific return, with model owner initiating integration and assignee choosing the concrete repair. No independent agent-owned S2-specific attenuation policy is claimed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-run isolated branches/worktrees, Change and ordered revisions, integratechange, conditional transitions, qualityiteration and idempotent run enqueue. - Closure path: Parallel sub-goal outputs → owner integrates one into shared branch → subsequent integration conflict → service marks conflict and enqueues affected assignee → assignee reworks against newer base → new revision becomes ready → owner retries integration. - Distinct S1 units: Concurrent independently assigned sub-goal implementation runs, each with its own assignee/worktree and Change against the same goal branch. - Inter-S1 disturbance: A previous sub-goal&#39;s integration changes the shared base, making another independently completed change incompatible at merge. - Attenuating coordination relation: Conflict-specific MarkChangeIntegrated(false) returns the disrupted sub-goal to rework instead of accepting stale output; Change revision history preserves the old failure/new base relation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The assignee receives a successor run and prompt text explicitly identifying the prior conflicting Change and requirement to resolve it on the new integration base. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: A concrete disturbance is generated between independently produced S1 changes, then causes a targeted adjustment in the affected peer&#39;s subsequent work; static worktree isolation alone is not the positive witness. - Boundary reachability: The documented sub-goal/change-integration workflow invokes MarkChangeIntegrated on failed Git integration, with the recovery path wired into normal owner/assignee runs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The relation is first-party fixed conflict policy (C), while agent workers provide the actual revision and owner chooses when/what to merge; no general independent S2 judgment actor is shown. - Evidence: collaboration conflict contract, conflict transition/rework, assignee conflict wake. - Basis: explicit + structural constructor control with subsequent operational return. - Confidence: High for specific first-party conflict-loop mechanics. - Caveats: C does not assert a free-standing model coordinator that negotiates all possible peer conflicts; ordinary consult/handoff alone are not counted as S2.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Own and regulate goal-wide current commitments — sub-goal assignment, integration/recovery and handover — with a separate legitimate human intervention mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing incomplete sub-goals, failed verifications, ready/conflicting changes, stalled owner runs, disputed acceptance and human-requested rework. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Agent owner may choose assignees/sub-goals, request help, transfer ownership and integrate ready changes from a current goal-wide state; parent human may redirect/assign and reject a goal in review, returning binding correction to the owner. - Decision owner: Agent/squad leader owner model in autonomous mode (A) under goal-role authority; human operator acting through review/assignment UI/API in parent mode (P). Coordinator derives attention but does not make these substantive decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Goal/sub-goal/change/verification SQLite state, role tokens and requireOwnerOf, owner-only CLI, event-driven OwnerAttention, guarded single owner run, human review state and feedback comments. - Closure path: Goal/sub-goal/change status → derived owner attention and current context → owner model allocates or integrates operational commitments → assignee execution and results → persisted state and next owner wake. Parent mode: goal-wide review evidence → human approve/reject/redirect → binding review transition → a rejected goal creates a new owner run with explicit human reason, or approval triggers delivery. - Whole-system current view: The owner can list goal/sub-goals/changes/verification and sees an actionable wake summary; human UI exposes goal progress, review and changed artifacts. This is the whole declared goal, not an unverified cross-domain fleet command center. - Current-control decision scope: Assigning work to specific S1 units, integrating/reworking their results, changing owner accountability, deciding continuation vs accepted delivery and returning human rejection priorities into next operation; distinct from static concurrency caps. - Boundary reachability: README and Collaboration.v2.md prescribe owner-only subgoal/integration/handoff tools; service rejects non-owner use, wakes owner after actionable changes and records human review decisions via ResolveReview. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The connected owner model owns substantive task division and integration decisions in agent-assigned mode. The human&#39;s accepted/rejected review and reassignment are distinct authorized parent interventions; the daemon only enforces transitions.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Separately assigned agent verifier checks a sub-goal&#39;s actual output from an independent stable snapshot and can reject work before owner integration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: An assignee reports completed while produced code/result violates the sub-goal&#39;s declared requirements or quality expectations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Named verifier agent issues structured passed or rejected with evidence through verifysubgoal; rejected verdict prevents Change admission and enqueues another assignee run. - Decision owner: Explicitly configured distinct verifierid model acting in role=verify; platform authenticates verdict to that named verifier and owns deterministic state transition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Detached Git snapshot for verifier, separate verify run identity, no owner role authority, persisted verificationresult, agent-specific verdict tool and assignee rework loop. - Closure path: Assignee completes and source revision is frozen → verification run inspects stable snapshot → verifier model returns verdict/evidence → platform records result → rejected reopens task and enqueues the assignee with rejection summary, passed creates a ready Change for owner. - Claim being audited: The assignee&#39;s produced sub-goal deliverable is complete/valid enough to become an integrable Change. - Ordinary reporting path: Assignee&#39;s run completion, summary, commits and domain machine-check output recorded on the goal and sub-goal. - Complementary access path: Dedicated verifier run in a detached snapshot of the submitted sub-goal branch can inspect real work/files and issue independent verdict/evidence; named verifier&#39;s verifysubgoal call, not assignee self-report, closes the audit. - Independence boundary: Distinct configured verifier agent/worktree/run/role versus assignee, with a stable detached submitted ref. Service verifies verifier identity, status and running verdict window; a single-actor configuration or default machine verification does not demonstrate independent agent audit. The code validates the named verifierid but does not categorically forbid configuring it equal to assignee ID, so independence must be configured. - Who acts on findings: Service immediately transitions rejected sub-goals into new assignee work with qualityiteration increment and rejection summary injected; passed work goes to owner integration. Squad reviewer comments are separately human-advisory and are not the source of this positive claim. - Boundary reachability: The subgoal API supports verifierid, daemon dispatches role=verify on a stable source snapshot and VerifySubGoal commits the verdict plus subsequent correction run. This is a first-party standard optional mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model verifier decides the substantive completion judgment on independent source evidence; routine machine verification/guardrails are supporting checks only. - Evidence: sub-goal creation/verifier ID, verdict authority and rework, detached verifier workspace. - Basis: explicit + structural complementary audit mode. - Confidence: High for first-party configured verifier control, medium for actual independence when an operator does not select distinct agents. - Caveats: Default verifier is machine-command-based and not a positive agent S3 by itself; human review of squad opinions remains the human&#39;s verdict. Role-level sandbox relies on first-party worktree/role implementation and external execution environments.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No established agent-owned or parent-closed prospective adaptation of the domain/goal organization&#39;s future capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New tracker issues, schedules, acceptance failures and historical task results affect current/recurring work, not a decision on future organization design. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human defines domain policy and runtime/agent configuration; a processor can compile natural-language acceptance rules, but has no authority to choose prospective strategic changes in a future environmental model. - Decision owner: Operator for domain acceptance/agent roster; active agents for present work only. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Policy-text compilation to deterministic checks, history, gate statistics, cron/issue triggers and domain reporting. - Closure path: Human policy → checks/gates → operational verification/delivery; schedule → clone current goal. Neither is environmental futures → adaptation options → strategic selection → reconfiguration return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Acceptance-policy compilation and recurring triggers are present control configuration, not prospective intelligence about external future requirements. - Evidence: policy and trigger overview, domain policy configuration. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A human may use historical results to redesign future capabilities outside this bounded first-party loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No declared closed ultimate-purpose/identity arbitration authority beyond human domain acceptance and operational gates. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unapproved side effects, assignee role violation, poor task quality and agent handoff loops are bounded by service/human gates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human may define domain acceptance, approve/reject a goal or stop a handoff loop; those are goal acceptance/operational continuation decisions rather than enduring identity/purpose arbitration. - Decision owner: Single human operator for policy configuration and review actions; deterministic service for permission checks. No recurring S5 issue adjudicator distinct from S3 control. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Goal ownership authority, role tokens, acceptance-policy freeze, human checkpoint, handoff-loop guard and audit logs. - Closure path: Human config → operational acceptance checks/permissions, or review → goal rejection/approval → next run/delivery; no mission/identity conflict → legitimate ultimate-policy judgment → changed binding constitution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Human final approval does not automatically imply VSM S5; even a domain policy string is an execution constraint rather than an ultimate organizational identity dispute loop. - Evidence: domain/goal policy, review resolution, role authority. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Ultimate identity/purpose decisions may exist in the real operator&#39;s institution, outside this open-source single-user software boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1bce7f1a2ff3c599516a40edbd064de106784076/assessments/ag/agentwork.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1bce7f1a2ff3c599516a40edbd064de106784076\">1bce7f1 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T12:59:07+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Orchestragent/orchestragent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">orch</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Orchestragent/orchestragent/tree/533d06b440654e0f9c9654c80945a751796747e5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 533d06b440654e0f9c9654c80945a751796747e5\">533d06b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Generate and repair code in response to scoped user specification and feedback from tests and model calls. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfamiliar source, incomplete interfaces, implementation errors, rejected model outputs and failing frozen tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose a task solution/patch from specification, context and feedback, including changed implementation on failure. - Decision owner: Model-backed planner and builder calls in the installed run; deterministic code enforces schema, attempts and test acceptance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Worktrees, contextpack, prompt budgets, frozen tests, provider adaptors, retry ladder. - Closure path: Model plan → task/test contracts → model patch → sandbox test → accept or issue failure feedback → reattempt/replan/park. - Boundary reachability: plan and execute are first-party orchestrator operations reachable through supported CLI. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The planner and code model choose substantive task decomposition and edits; the scheduler does not author those solutions. - Evidence: orchrun.py#L715-L885, orchrun.py#L3247-L3477, orchrun.py#L3848-L3905. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for a reachable source-level loop; no provider-run success reproduced. - Caveats: The normal run requires configured model inference and an executable project environment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Distinct S1 units: Parallel independently model-backed task builders, each operating in its own task-specific worktree against the same shared integration branch. - Inter-S1 disturbance: A builder&#39;s code or contract can overlap another builder&#39;s changed files, produce a Git conflict, or invalidate a frozen test already passed by another task. - Attenuating coordination relation: Planned file ownership/dependencies, serialized merge lock, affected-tests integration gate and explicit capped rebasing of collided tasks. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Conflict or affected-test failure returns the named task to READY/rebase or retry with updated branch state rather than silently accepting both edits. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The implemented merge-collision-to-task-retry and cross-task test-interference gate regulate actual interaction failures between distinct coding units. - Function: Attenuate interference between parallel code-building units in a shared integration branch. - Disturbance / variety regulated: File ownership overlap, incompatible dependency interfaces, parallel Git conflicts and tests broken by another worker&#39;s merge. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Coordinate dependency/ownership and merge order, reject a conflicting commit and route the affected task back for a refreshed build. - Decision owner: Deterministic DAG scheduler, interface and file gates and integration merge lock; no separate autonomous coordination actor owns the arbitration policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Planned dependency edges, serialized integration, affected-test gate, rebase count and changed-worktree tests. - Closure path: Concurrent task outputs → merge collision/affected tests → reject/rebase/retry of specific task → next task execution on updated integration state. - Boundary reachability: execute, ready, merge are standard paths for concurrent task runs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The collision/feedback witness is function-specific and shipped, but the coordinator&#39;s decisive scheduling/merge arbitration is hard-coded; agent-owned S2 would need composition. - Evidence: orchrun.py#L2170-L2257, orchrun.py#L2420-L2475, orchrun.py#L3848-L3910. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Mere DAG edges would not establish S2 without the identified collision-response/return path.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Whole-system current view: The run&#39;s persisted task table, DONE/READY/RUNNING/PARKED statuses, worker occupancy, rate/spend counters and outstanding dependency graph. - Current-control decision scope: Adjust current concurrency and admissions, reorder ready work by dependency criticality, or stop a module with repeated parked tasks; all are currently code-controlled rather than model-owned decisions. - Function: Control the complete current spec-run portfolio&#39;s admissions, throughput, resource limits and stalled tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider rate-limit pressure, too many active model calls, spend caps, blocked dependencies and repeating module failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Change current concurrency, dispatch priority and task admissions or park a failed module based on observed whole-run state. - Decision owner: Deterministic scheduler (drain, room, throttle, breaker); models generate tasks but do not autonomously decide whole-system resource intervention. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AIMD limit, token/cache policies, dependency depth sorting, persistent state, failure counts. - Closure path: Ready/in-flight state and failure signals → scheduler resource/priority intervention → changed dispatch and retry conditions for multiple tasks. - Boundary reachability: Standard execution uses drain and applies current maxjobs/maxverifyjobs, rate and spend gates. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A whole-run feedback and resource-decision path exists, but its discretionary control policy is fixed in code, leaving agent ownership uncomposed. - Evidence: orchrun.py#L2170-L2294, orchrun.py#L2451-L2490. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium; no autonomous allocator or qualified human parent mode evidenced. - Caveats: Ordinary user-set quotas do not independently establish P parent governance.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: The planner&#39;s frozen test contract correctly expresses the original user requirements, notwithstanding a builder&#39;s failing implementation or allegation of wrong tests. - Ordinary reporting path: The builder&#39;s candidate code and sandbox failure result are reported to the retry ladder and orchestration state. - Complementary access path: A separately prompted Claude reviewer receives original specification, requirement mapping, frozen test sources and explicit dispute, not just the builder&#39;s success report. - Independence boundary: Separate persistent review ID, pinned input hash and validated testscorrect/testswrong verdict; the builder cannot directly rewrite frozen tests. - Who acts on findings: review closes a correct-test dispute or fixtests commits guarded test corrections and re-verifies affected candidate and prior merges. - Function: Independently challenge the frozen test contract against the original user specification when task failures indicate a test dispute. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Wrong or over-constraining tests drafted before implementation, misleading failure attribution and builder attempts to relax constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: An exception-review model independently returns testscorrect or testswrong, with an explanation and guarded test edits; a wrong-test verdict can authorize corrective test revision. - Decision owner: Separate Claude review invocation using original specification, protected from the builder&#39;s own test-edit decision; deterministic code validates the verdict and safe application. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Immutable review inputs, durable review store, assertion-count no-weakening gate and regression check of already merged tasks. - Closure path: Failed worker/test dispute → independent review → validated finding → corrected frozen tests or rejection → saved candidate reverified / task retried. - Boundary reachability: review and fixtests are first-party retry-ladder operations; their execution is conditional, not an asserted always-on audit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewer has the substantive adjudication right; the builder cannot directly alter the pre-frozen test contract, while code checks and transports the verdict. - Evidence: orchrun.py#L3010-L3140, orchreview.py. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for the dispute-triggered complementary path. - Caveats: Independence is functional/prompt-and-role separation, not a demonstrated separate provider organization; ordinary final pytest gates alone would not establish A.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - External distinction: Past external project specifications, sandbox/test outcomes and model-builder failures vary between runs; rule candidates are grounded in these encountered environmental distinctions, not only an internal heartbeat. - Future / prospective distinction: The A/B and replay checks compare candidate rules against distinct past specifications to select instructions expected to improve subsequent planning/building runs. - Adaptation option generated: Model-authored, cross-task reusable planner or builder lessons become candidate rules, tested with/without them and potentially activated at later checkpoints. - Path back into current capability / S3: Promoted rules enter the planner or builder prompt layer in a subsequent run, changing the model&#39;s future action repertoire, while harmful rules are retired. - Function: Learn prospective planning/building-rule changes from accumulated cross-run failures and outcomes, and return successful changes into future model prompts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Repeating planning defects, weak tests, recurring builder mistakes and the possibility that a proxy for eventual success becomes misleading. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model review/replanning/build attempts formulate generalizable lessons; A/B evidence evaluates whether a candidate helps, with checkpoint promotion and subsequent prompt influence. - Decision owner: Autonomous models propose the adaptive rule content from task observations; deterministic promotion statistics retain activation veto, so the agent does not own final promotion discretion in isolation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Rule store, paired trials and replay, proxy-vs-real Spearman trust test, lifecycle candidate → active → retired and prompt layer budget. - Closure path: Outcomes and model lessons from earlier runs → candidate rule → with/without evaluation → promote/retire at checkpoint → altered next-plan/builder instruction. - Boundary reachability: learn, evolve, settleplannerrules and builder rule trials are first-party modes (automatic rule learning disabled in frozen-policy modes). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Autonomous models originate strategic adaptation content, not merely execute a preselected fixed retry; promotion is a statistical safeguard rather than a model-authored option. - Evidence: orchrun.py#L1570-L1775, orchrun.py#L3888-L3925, orchrules.py#L1-L125. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium for prospectively reusable operating-policy learning; causal effectiveness not independently reproduced. - Caveats: Feedback comes chiefly from past run/task outcomes, not market sensing. If an evaluator requires a wholly independent outside-environment sensor rather than cross-run experience, S4 should be revisited; the observed loop is durable future capability change, not just current repair.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party whole-system identity or ultimate-policy dispute and legitimate-authority closure is evidenced for a spec-run organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe workspace edits, mismatched model routes, insufficient budgets and altered test contracts are addressed as operational guardrails. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Bind an ultimate policy/identity judgment to later operations; no such accountable agent or legitimate parent governance loop is shipped. - Decision owner: User chooses configuration and optional frozen policy; model reviews resolve tests and task strategy, not organizational identity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Policy store, sandbox boundary, explicit model settings, pinned planning inputs, cancel/park limits and review gate. - Closure path: Configuration and safety gates constrain a run; no S5-level issue/appeal → authoritative decision → returned binding identity policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Guarded model choices and an immutable execution policy are not themselves an S5 constitutional decision. - Evidence: orchpolicy.py, orchrun.py#L715-L815. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Maintainer policy and a human&#39;s optional local run selection are outside this selected recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/987160f64eccd59b120a491c9f00becefacad4b3/assessments/or/orchestragent-orch.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 987160f64eccd59b120a491c9f00becefacad4b3\">987160f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T14:25:35+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mimshins/ork\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ork</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mimshins/ork/tree/e97d75c554095ff41d79ef91fafbbdc8bcfc8b64\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e97d75c554095ff41d79ef91fafbbdc8bcfc8b64\">e97d75c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Claude sessions in a workflow transform requests into code, specifications, reviews and other local project outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user requests, repository state, test failures, tool results and child reports within each session&#39;s bounded task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose concrete implementation/review actions and revise work from the tool, branch and report feedback. - Decision owner: model-driven Claude agent node in the first-party runtime; an operator supplies the initial task and permissions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: graph activation, SDK/CLI driver, tool allowlists, worktrees, event store, session budget and schema validation. - Closure path: agent chooses tools and edits, receives tool/result feedback, changes work and returns a structured report to its graph/leader. - Boundary reachability: claude.worker/claude.leader nodes and their tool/report bridge are supported installed node types, exercised structurally by shipped example workflows; no third-party agent&#39;s hidden control architecture is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: actions in a live session remain discretionary even though workflow graph edges, budgets and output transport are deterministic. - Evidence: scheduler built-ins and delegation, delegation port, factory example. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider execution was not performed in this review; operator credentials and the chosen workflow are prerequisites.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: regulate competing write access among parallel S1 sessions in the same run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two concurrent writing agents can attempt overlapping files/folders or shared named resources, introducing overwrites or merge conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: agents select resources to claim, release them and respond to a refused claim by choosing another task or negotiating with the holder; the registry deterministically detects overlap. - Decision owner: agent session for claim selection and the corrective work choice; deterministic claim registry for overlap verdict, holder identity, event delivery and automatic release. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: advisory claim registry keyed by normalized paths, refusal and release notices, worktree isolation and message channels. - Closure path: a conflicting claim is refused with holder identity; the agent receives that response, can communicate/replan, and may retry after a release notification, changing the subsequent S1 work target. - Boundary reachability: claim/release tools are supplied to writing Claude nodes when a run exposes parallel writers; the scheduler binds the run-scoped claim port. This is a shipped operating mode, not a hypothetical custom integration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Ork does not choose the agent&#39;s alternative work in response to contention; its registry only determines the fixed overlap rule. Claim discipline is advisory, so not every actual edit is protected. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently running writer leader/worker nodes or spawned worker copies with separate tasks and checkouts. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping resource/path edits and resultant destructive interference, specifically documented as the reason for claims. - Attenuating coordination relation: explicit per-resource claim refusal, release, holder/contender notification and optional peer messaging. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the refused agent sees the holding node and can work elsewhere or ask it; release notice tells the agent to claim again before editing. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: resource overlap is checked in executable first-party code, with a conflict-specific refusal/release feedback loop; message passing is only a support channel. - Evidence: claim registry implementation, Claude claim tool, claims and message specification. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: autonomous conflict handling is agent-instructed but not hard-enforced and was not exercised with a provider here; if the claimed function is defined solely as lock acquisition, that deterministic subdecision is not agent-owned.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: control a multi-child run&#39;s current commitments and exceptions at a configured leader/whole-run boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: child failure, run budget/concurrency saturation, exhausted revision loops, and conflicting current delivery commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the leader can choose whether and where to delegate/retry in light of child reports; an owner can answer an exhausted-loop exception, while configuration author sets the initial hard limits. - Decision owner: first-party leader/owner decision ports are available; workflow author must compose a genuinely whole-run supervisory objective and authority envelope. The deterministic runner owns enforcement, not the resource-policy judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: slot/budget accounting, maxWorkers cap, statuses, reports, loop counter, human fallback and halt semantics. - Closure path: delegated children return outcomes and costs; the leader can select follow-on work, or answer an exhausted loop to stop/extend/go onward; the scheduler executes the selected transition. - Boundary reachability: leader tools, child reports and owner-question/answer loop are shipped in the scheduler and Claude runtime; their S3-specific whole-run composition depends on the declared workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: these are real current-intervention primitives, but a generic leader&#39;s task division alone does not establish a default autonomous whole-run S3 owner for arbitrary user workflows. This supports the narrower constructor C, not universal A. - Whole-system current view: available child reports, run status, costs and configured limits; whether they form an actionable whole-system view is workflow-dependent. - Current-control decision scope: child commitments and retry/loop continuation under configured budgets; no assertion that the agent can revise operator-specified global policy. - Evidence: leader/child delegation, loop owner decision, scheduler budget. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: no parent-mode suffix from ordinary human gates, pause/stop or local approval; no runtime end-to-end current-control trace tested.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: inspect implementation claims through separately tasked agents that can directly read the produced branch rather than rely only on the builder&#39;s report. - Disturbance / variety regulated: defects in the builder&#39;s asserted completion, including security, correctness, UX and code-quality issues overlooked by ordinary production. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independent readonly reviewer sessions judge the produced work and return pass/changesrequested; the builder then receives review status and revises in a bounded loop. - Decision owner: separate reviewer Claude agents in the first-party software-factory opt-in mode; graph routing enforces returned verdicts, but is not the audit judge. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: readonly checkout, fresh reviewer sessions, join statuses, bounded edge maxRounds, agent approver and optional human publication gate. - Closure path: builder branch is inspected by separately running security/UX/code reviewers; a changesrequested result routes back to builder and triggers repair; passing reviews route to the approver. - Boundary reachability: examples/software-factory.ork.yaml is a distributed executable Ork workflow invoking the installed reviewer node types with readonly project access. The path is an opt-in mode, not assumed present in every workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer models decide the audit verdict based on direct artifact access; deterministic joins merely transport outcomes. Their separation is context/process isolation, not institutional independence from the operator or model supplier. - Claim being audited: builder&#39;s assertion that a branch implements the spec correctly and safely. - Ordinary reporting path: builder&#39;s status, summary and artifact/branch report to the workflow. - Complementary access path: security/UX/code agents in readonly workspaces inspect the actual branch and surrounding code, not just the builder&#39;s summary. - Independence boundary: different agent sessions, reviewer roles and readonly source access; all are authored within one user-defined workflow, so a malicious author can weaken the separation. - Who acts on findings: builder corrects the branch when any review requests changes; approver examines the passing result before the user-controlled publication step. - Evidence: factory workflow, PR-review workflow, worktree/delegation behavior. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: source-grounded installed example path, not independently executed S3 evaluation or a universal independent-audit service; routine sequential QA in an arbitrary workflow would not automatically qualify.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party externally oriented prospective adaptation loop is established at the assessed run boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing external demands or future capabilities could require adaptation, but no shipped owner closes that function. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an Ork-run S4. - Decision owner: none demonstrated; workflow authors or maintainers may revise YAML and Ork code outside the run. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: captured feedback, event store, local feedback list/note/dismiss, context compiler and user-authored scripts. - Closure path: no shipped feedback-to-accepted-change/lesson path into future current run capability at this revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: collection and grouping of past errors are not themselves a future-oriented model/options/return loop. - Evidence: feedback spec with explicit partial implementation, feedback guide, roadmap. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a user may build a separate adaptation workflow; that is not shipped S4 closure in the declared default system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy adjudication path for a workflow organization is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: identity or policy tensions can arise, but Ork&#39;s gates and constraints concern routine task/runtime permissions and delivery. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped S5-specific issue, legitimate authority judgment and return-to-operation path. - Decision owner: workflow author/operator defines configuration and controls ordinary approval; those actions are not an evidenced identity-policy process. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: YAML settings, tool allowlists, budget caps, askuser, human gates, CLI run controls and local auth. - Closure path: ordinary approvals/stop and permission decisions affect run progression, not an established ultimate-policy matter. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a user or agent can edit instruction text or choose publication, but no system-specific S5 right is constructed or closed. - Evidence: workflow format, message and decision controls, factory human gate. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the external human/project may have its own legitimate S5; Ork&#39;s default standard distribution does not supply that governance loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/06adb32ca65514fbe9a0f7c625cdfe64b2c702de/assessments/or/ork.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 06adb32ca65514fbe9a0f7c625cdfe64b2c702de\">06adb32 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T17:43:47+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DarkDvr/Synapse\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Synapse</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DarkDvr/Synapse/tree/b91345750a7d8ec1fca1557cdf33613762794223\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b91345750a7d8ec1fca1557cdf33613762794223\">b913457 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Autonomous local production and checking of a specific delegated project deliverable. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Ambiguous requirements, codebase/tool errors, rendered defects and incomplete subtasks encountered during task execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Role model chooses its own next implementation/test tool after observing results. - Decision owner: Role-specific model agent, reached through Synapse-created engine session; human owns separate scope/permission gates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OpenCode adapter, per-role tool rules, session brief, tool transcript, provider retries, bounded worker runner and written-artifact ledger. - Closure path: Model tool call→real engine execution→tool feedback→continuing role turn→worker report/files, then return to Architect. - Boundary reachability: Shipped Multi mode makes genuine model-backed role sessions rather than invoking a development mock. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The role&#39;s model has task-sensitive tool/repair choice; Synapse supplies the enclosing execution and evidence channel. - Evidence: OpenCode adapter, runs.js, plans.js. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high on reachable code path; no independent Windows-provider execution. - Caveats: Actual model quality, installed/packaged engine availability and end-to-end completion were not live-tested.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Feedback-based regulation of interference or oscillation among operational S1 task agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent edits, shared project plan corruption and worker cross-role overwrite risks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No specific agent-owned inter-worker collision detection→mediation→returned adjustment evidenced. - Decision owner: Deterministic harness serialization and folder rules; Architect decides task sequence as S3 commitment, not evidenced as S2 conflict mediation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: One-active-worker busy guard per project, roleWriteRules, plan ownership, role folders and assignment ladder. - Closure path: Serial admission and static write rules prevent some conflicts but do not inspect actual inter-unit interference and negotiate mutual change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Transport, isolation and top-down task allocation alone are insufficient for S2 at this recursion. - Evidence: runs.js#L546, plans.js. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for the reviewed first-party orchestration paths. - Caveats: A model could discuss competing tasks in an individual session, but no reusable S2-specific interference closure was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: Architect reads the project-wide plan.json task statuses, blocked items, confirmed features and per-worker completion reports grounded in runs.jsonl and actual file/tool records. - Current-control decision scope: Architect alone accepts/rejects task completion, alters the whole plan and selects corrective work and next role commitments within approved project scope; a runner&#39;s retry/backoff merely enforces constraints. - Function: Current project-wide commitment, progress and corrective task regulation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Partial deliverables, faulty worker claims, blocked tasks, failed providers, scope drift and new defects threatening the approved project goal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Judge each reported worker outcome, update the plan, mark done/retry/blocked, reprioritize or delegate the next current task. - Decision owner: Model-driven Architect in Multi mode, constrained by operator-approved features and optional automatic continuation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan.json as Architect-only authority, actual runs.jsonl, injected post-worker report, task-role assignment, retry fallback, progress/status rail. - Closure path: Worker run evidence→synthetic report→Architect evaluation/update of plan→next delegate or required operator escalation; Keep building enables continuation, while off returns after each judged task. - Boundary reachability: delegate MCP, run ledger, handleTurn, report and wake are connected in shipped Multi mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the Architect leaves recorded runs and deterministic stops but not discretionary whole-project acceptance, replanning or corrective commitment. - Evidence: runs.js#L2741, plans.js#L1270, delegate MCP. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high; project-control closure is code-visible, not a live success claim. - Caveats: An operator can pause or steer a task, but the documented toggles do not independently establish a distinct parent-governed S3 decision mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: Implementing worker&#39;s claim that the shipped build is usable, faithful to approved design and free of target regressions. - Ordinary reporting path: Coder sends its own transcript, modified-file list and completion statement to Architect through runs.jsonl and the synthetic result message. - Complementary access path: Separate QC model role uses the delivered application, renders/screenshots UI, diffs approved mock, tests unusual inputs and writes reproducible findings with IDs independently of worker prose. - Independence boundary: QC has its own configurable model/session and role folder; it cannot edit the build and coders cannot write the QC findings folder under Synapse&#39;s first-party role rules. This is role separation, not tamper-proof institutional independence. - Who acts on findings: Architect reads QC output and delegates the numbered fix to a coder, then assigns verification of that exact repro to QC again, up to the three-round cap. - Function: Independent role-level challenge to implementation claims using real build behavior and a returned quality verdict. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Worker reports that conceal unusable UI, clipping, misleading states, poor validation or regressions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: QC chooses and executes adversarial/use-oriented checks, records reproducible findings and re-verifies fixes separately from implementing coders. - Decision owner: Dedicated configured qc model/session, distinct from coder role, with its own findings area and read-only stance toward the build. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: QC mandate to render/use the built artifact, screenshot/mock diffs, finding IDs and repros, separate folder permissions, bounded three-round QC/fix/verification plan. - Closure path: QC direct inspection→qcfindings/qc-N.md with evidence→Architect receives report and assigns specific fix task→QC re-runs repros and verifies correction. - Boundary reachability: First-party QC role, delegated real engine session, report loop and role-write restrictions are shipped in Multi mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: QC independently determines and reports actual product defects; the Architect, not QC, retains current-control commitment changes. - Evidence: plans.js#L700, plans.js#L1342, runs.js#L1002. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium: independently tasked source-visible QC path, with no performed cross-provider execution or efficacy audit. - Caveats: The product does not establish strong organizational or cryptographic isolation; different role/model configuration and artifact-based repro constitute the limited claimed independence. If QC is never delegated in a particular plan, the optional path is inactive.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Sensing external/future needs and developing options returned to current project capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unanticipated user needs and future features for the product under construction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: An Inventor model proposes and ranks extra features; operator chooses scope and Architect links confirmed features to tasks. Whether this amounts to an external sensing→adaptation→S3 loop is unresolved. - Decision owner: Inventor generates options and operator accepts features; no proved future-environment evidence trail connecting the two. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Inventor role mandate, optional local search tools, ideas file, confirmed/rejected features.json and Architect planning instructions. - Closure path: Proposed feature→human selection→confirmed feature/task is explicit; source does not establish that the proposal derived from an observed external prospective distinction rather than imagined additions to a current wish. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Generating project feature ideas is not, alone, a demonstrated VSM S4 process; the active inventor path prevents a defensible categorical absence claim. - Evidence: plans.js#L660, plans.js#L1287, README. - Basis: structural - Confidence: bounded uncertainty. - Caveats: Direct evidence of actual external future-condition observation and its governing choice would resolve the state; neither was reproduced at this source pin.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Legitimate ultimate-policy or system identity dispute resolution at project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicts over project identity, ultimate purpose and governing policy rather than current task scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party identity-level issue→authority decision→returned governing policy path shown. - Decision owner: Human operator chooses product features, permission and continuity toggles; those remain operational/requirement decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Feature selection forms, prompt constraints, role-specific permission rules and operator approval. - Closure path: Scope selection reaches project plan, but no recognized ultimate-policy decision channel governs a dispute about the system&#39;s identity. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Choosing which mock to build or whether to continue is not an S5 legitimacy mechanism. - Evidence: plans.js#L1287, automation.js, permissions.js. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: External real-world product governance or developer organizational decisions are out of the assessed standard-distribution boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7211b7addb042e94e57c4d3d7934a31e2d314368/assessments/da/darkdvr-synapse.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7211b7addb042e94e57c4d3d7934a31e2d314368\">7211b7a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T17:22:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/HG-ha/Argusai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ArgusAI</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/HG-ha/Argusai/tree/5f7aa0844ae0341f3563da2a4d171e3fb797d3ac\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5f7aa0844ae0341f3563da2a4d171e3fb797d3ac\">5f7aa08 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out security-analysis and general assistant operations through model-selected tools and separate task-performing children. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain code/traffic observations, new user materials, scan errors, tool outputs, environment and evidence changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select and sequence domain tools, analyze returned evidence and choose next investigation/validation/report action. - Decision owner: Configured native model-bearing ArgusAI assistant, or connected model-bearing child where given an explicit bounded role; external ACP engines are not credited as ArgusAI&#39;s independent cognitive design. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model/tool call loop, domain tool catalog, run/stream state, content archives, confirmation broker and scoped child capability projection. - Closure path: User-defined scope → model/tool call on live workspace → task/evidence output → model inspects and continues/terminates → report or next task state. - Boundary reachability: The native model/tool engine and first-party domain registry are wired into normal GUI/CLI operation, and child tool selection is registered in the shipped assistant. An external ACP model is an alternative supported operating mode, not required for the native witness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses substantive task actions; deterministic policy, progress guards and run queues enforce limits rather than owning the choice. - Evidence: assistant loop, tool methods, child execution, README runtime. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for model/tool-path existence; provider execution was not replayed here. - Caveats: A standalone scan engine or GUI table does not own S1 without the configured model session.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: Independently executing persistent Team Room members, each with distinct objective, tool projection, history, inbox and ongoing work; temporary subagents also exist but do not communicate directly. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Repeated peer status chatter loops, duplicate or out-of-order targeted requests, interruption of busy specialists, concurrent competition for shared member capacity and missed handoff dependencies. - Attenuating coordination relation: Team member system context requires model discretion to respond only when its role/objective is implicated, to make concrete @role handoffs and otherwise call teamroompass. Durable inboxes deduplicate input references, queue while busy and preserve explicit request ordering; capacity waiters resume as slots release. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: A specialist reviews actual targeted peer/leader messages after finishing its current work, chooses to pass, answer with new evidence or issue an exact-role request; that message enters peer inboxes and changes their next work/action choice. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The explicit model-facing anti-loop rule and selective peer-response/handoff control mitigate inter-agent chatter/attention oscillation, while delivery sequencing/dedup solves concrete concurrent-message interference. A generic chat log alone would not establish the agent-owned decision. - Function: Maintain compatible peer coordination without interrupt/reply storms across simultaneous team work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Redundant chat cycles, missed role-specific requests, reentrant live work and shared member-slot interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide when own role must answer an inter-agent request, pass unproductive chatter, or send a bounded dependency/handoff to another specialist. - Decision owner: Model-bearing persistent member agents exercising model-facing Team Room rules; deterministic queue/dedup/capacity checks only supply delivery discipline. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Separate Team Room log/cursors and pending-input queue, selective @role message route, teamroompass, input reference dedup and member concurrency limits. - Closure path: Peer message/redundant chatter or directed dependency → model member evaluates role fit → pass or targeted correction/handoff → queue records/limits the message → affected member consumes it and adjusts work. - Boundary reachability: Persistent Team Rooms, first-party member prompt and member tools are shipped in the standard GUI/assistant mode; they are not optional third-party skills. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The decision whether to escalate/pass/answer is model discretion. Serial/held input alone would not support A, so the positive is restricted to the standing-team model-facing selective coordination path. - Evidence: agent role prompt and member queue, Team Room routing and read cursors, architecture/queue contract. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; no empirical measured chat-loop reduction is claimed. - Caveats: Temporary spawnteam has no peer messaging; this S2 A is confined to the separately supported persistent-team mode, not all single-agent sessions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Whole-system current view: Parent assistant can read the complete Todo tree, current task/job progress, team roster, child notices, evidence links and tool outputs; owner GUI sees team state and can edit/steer running membership and assignments. - Current-control decision scope: Model chooses investigation phases, work assignments, task continuation, relevant members, tool batches and response to child results. Owner separately changes standing-team staffing, objective/role assignments and active scope of current work, with next-turn effect. - Function: Coordinate immediate work commitments and evidence production for a whole user objective. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Specialist readiness, task phase dependencies, uncertain evidence and external tool errors, changing user direction, busy child agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model creates/updates whole-goal Todo plan, delegates and redirects workers; parent owner can edit or pause team members, change scope and direct next assigned work. - Decision owner: Main configured model for autonomous management; installation owner via Team GUI/member update for the parent mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Todo plan, run locks, live UI, child registry, Team Room and group message stores, confirmation/permission gates. - Closure path: Whole-goal evidence and child notices → model changes priorities/delegation → different agent/tool actions; owner observes team, edits member/assignments or sends new instruction → persisted metadata and injected typed event change subsequent child operation. - Boundary reachability: Both assistant model tools (settodos, spawnteam, agent input) and owner GUI team/member edit controls are first-party installed entry points; they return into running agent sessions rather than isolated dashboards. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Autonomous model retains current resource choices; the user separately holds a legitimate parent current-control role over the same local team. Hard permissions/queues alone do not count as a second owner. - Evidence: model planning/delegation methods, child control/notifications, owner team/member editing. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high within installed modes; no actual provider-backed orchestration run was executed. - Caveats: User task interjections are not credited alone; the distinct parent mode is the current-control owner GUI plus persistent member state returned to operation.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: Distinct independent examination of operational claims via complementary raw reality rather than ordinary task reporting; full closure cannot be established confidently from the reviewed default distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Misleading child report, inaccurate scan/finding, incomplete task claim. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently challenge a worker/result with raw Case/Artifact/Tool evidence and return a corrective finding to control; whether a governed non-producer audit owner routinely exercises it is unresolved. - Decision owner: Main assistant may inspect material and can instantiate readonly specialists; the built-in reporter role consolidates findings but is not shown to have a mandatory independently sourced veto/repair channel. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Case/Evidence original references, readonly subagents, result summaries, operator review, tool histories and durable event logs. - Closure path: Child or domain output → main assistant can reread raw evidence and assign further validation → correction to tasks is possible, but a shipped independent complementary inspection owner and repeatable corrective return are not fully proven. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Source supports model review as ordinary operations and a purpose-composable review role, yet cannot distinguish a true independent S3 audit from a task-specific secondary analysis in all supported modes. - Evidence: evidence/case store, main review path, readonly specialist presets/child roles. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium for real audit capability, low for verified S3-specific ownership. - Caveats: webaudit reporter summarizes other roles and the human inspects findings; neither automatically supplies independent unfiltered ground-truth inspection. No presumed external security review is credited.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Adapt future patrol priorities, scheduled investigation or long-lived operational capability from external change; specific closed adaptation ownership remains unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changed attack surface, new file/log events, future alerts and cross-session findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose and enact a prospective change to future monitoring/adaptation, distinct from simply completing current steps or running fixed Cron commands. - Decision owner: A model may call first-party cronupsert and alter future work triggers; an installation owner can also configure schedules. However no clearly evidenced shipped model process compares new environment trends, decides a durable revised future capability, and closes that decision back into later operating configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Cron tasks with interval/cron/webhook/file-change triggers, persisted jobs/run records, long-term session memory and task/Case references. - Closure path: External event → scheduled session inspects current artifacts and may update Todos/schedule → later trigger executes; the decisive model&#39;s prospective adaptation criterion and actual feedback-based reconfiguration are not resolved at this pin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model-selectable scheduler plus memory offers a credible first-party adaptation candidate, but scheduling fixed repetitions and reusing prior facts alone are not an established S4 owner. - Evidence: model-visible cronupsert catalog, persistent scheduler, scheduler GUI routes, long-term memory. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium for future triggers, insufficient for whether they constitute positive S4 adaptation. - Caveats: ? is caused by an identified plausible shipped path, not by a hypothetical future feature; fixed scheduling should not be mistaken for itself owning outside-and-then intelligence.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: What a persistent local Team is for, which named specialist roles constitute it, and which role objectives/tool-power boundaries bind members across sessions. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: The legitimate self-hosted installation/team owner can create, recharter and edit its standing team and member objectives through first-party GUI/API; no agent-owned constitutional authority to replace that owner&#39;s ultimate charter is established here. - Return-to-operation path: Owner changes standing team purpose or member role/objective/capabilities → persisted team/member record and pending typed metadata update → next member turn receives new role/objective; tool capability projection/confirmation mode enforces limits. - Function: Maintain binding purpose and role identity of the persistent team as a supported parent-governed arrangement. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changed organizational assignment, durable role constitution and owner-level policy over specialist identity and allowed operating repertoire. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Authoritatively change a team/member&#39;s stated continuing purpose, role identity and authorization scope for subsequent work, beyond changing a single tool call. - Decision owner: Installation owner in the supported human-operated GUI/team setup mode; model tools can request team changes but no distinct self-authorized autonomous ultimate-policy closure is claimed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persisted Team metadata, member records, edited role/objective, capability projection and queued metadataupdatepending typed event. - Closure path: Parent decides a standing team/role-purpose revision → updateteammember persists the charter and emits event → member&#39;s future context/inbox incorporates it → subsequent agent work follows updated purpose/policy. - Boundary reachability: Creating and updating persistent teams and members are shipped, first-party GUI/API features, not repository maintainer policy or an external integration. The member worker reads revised metadata on its next safe turn. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The credited S5 mode is owner-authored standing team constitution, not the assistant approving its own tools or choosing current task steps; S3-level task steering remains separately recorded. - Evidence: persistent team create/update, member metadata revision and next-turn notice, member role/permission API schema. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium for the local standing team identity loop. - Caveats: One-off job objectives, approval toggles and permission flags alone are not S5. This claim is limited to the persistent-team role/purpose constitution, not governance of unrelated ArgusAI installations or external security organizations.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/49cc3492e73efe3cbdcbc2bc63dac903deaa2cfd/assessments/ar/argusai.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 49cc3492e73efe3cbdcbc2bc63dac903deaa2cfd\">49cc349 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T22:45:47+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/andreseloysv/Tirith\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tirith</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/andreseloysv/Tirith/tree/be358146324a8a9c98e6649938205328160c79cf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision be358146324a8a9c98e6649938205328160c79cf\">be35814 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-operated coding and repository task execution in separately launched worktree lanes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown code changes, failing tools/tests, context limits and incomplete user-specified plan items. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select edits, terminal/repository operations and subsequent task actions based on observed tool results and brief constraints. - Decision owner: Configured coding-agent model operating each spawned lane; manager is another model-mediated operational conversation for on-call tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model CLI adapters, lane brief, isolated worktree, agent log reader, plan report, hooks, tool gates, retry and timeout bounds. - Closure path: Open plan item → manager/launcher starts coding-model CLI → model acts in worktree and observes results → reports done/partial/blocked → reviewer/manager feedback may cause a corrected new turn. - Boundary reachability: The shipping lane run/launch command builds supported first-party agent aliases and reads real provider output; plan gate and dispatcher route results into the active project plan. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Plan reservation, test gates and Git merge logic cannot choose the substantive code edit; only the model agent does. - Evidence: agent runner, launcher, launch contract. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for wired agent invocation and returned status. - Caveats: Agent binaries/provider accounts are external prerequisites; this review did not execute a paid live coding session.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Prevent duplicate concurrent ownership and overlapping write scopes between independent operational lanes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two plan workers claim the same item, write the same folder or operate on an incomplete prerequisite, causing stale or conflicting source changes. - Distinct S1 units: Multiple separately launched model-agent lanes in individual worktrees, possibly concurrent on separate tasks. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Same task ID can be claimed twice, and explicit write paths can overlap including ancestor/descendant folders. - Attenuating coordination relation: SQLite claim/attempt-token transaction, dependency check, lease/heartbeat, write-path overlap check and reported claim refusal; centralized merge queue serializes integration separately. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Conflicting claims block launch, a missing predecessor remains blocked until actually merged, and released/failed reservations become visible to new launcher/manager decisions. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: Path-specific atomic exclusivity and stale-attempt fencing address an identified inter-agent resource collision; failure and recovery change which subsequent S1 unit may begin work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Determine conflict admission and reclaim/release an expired claim after checking worker liveness. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic coordination constructor (SQLite rules and collector recovery), not a model that can change the reservation policy in the operating mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: coordination.sqlite3, permanent item IDs, explicit declared writes, lease and attempt token, heartbeat, worker inbox with idempotent messages. - Closure path: Candidate lane → transaction checks existing owners, prerequisites and write overlap → accepted lease or refused conflict → manager/launcher receives available work or blockage and revises later starts. - Boundary reachability: Standard launch acquires reservation before creating worktree/spawning agent; hook/report paths check attempt token; collector recovers dead-owner claims. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Models select task priorities and write declarations but the final conflict verdict, arbitration and lease recovery run deterministically. - Evidence: coordination implementation, coordination contract, merge queue. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for reservation conflicts in declared scopes. - Caveats: Legacy items without declared write paths have no additional path reservation; independent raw shell writes remain bounded by other tool/worktree gates, not this S2 path.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Regulate the current cross-project plan/worktree portfolio, agent assignments, blocked work, integration and escalation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open work without a worker, stalled/rejected reviews, failed stages, WIP congestion, exhausted agent credits and owner-inbox backlog. - Whole-system current view: Manager brief/queue and Tirith commands expose open plan items, active lanes, status, review/merge queue and recent reports; operator dashboard shows all projects, agents, pipeline results and current owner actions. - Current-control decision scope: Manager chooses split, launch, combine, wait, retry, drop, or ask operator about a current item and handles failed reviews; operator can manually plan-edit, launch/stop agents, switch autopilot and resolve pending current decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Base model manager selects current operational commitments across plan items; human parent can directly alter commitments and stop/relaunch under the installed owner UI. - Decision owner: Base A — persistent on-call manager model. Parent P — human owner acting through Tirith desktop/inbox/plan UI; collector enforces allowed decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Resumable manager session, supervisor queue, autopilot proposals, plan gate, lane launcher, merge WIP limits and auto-repair, user inbox and owner-only actions. - Closure path: Base: collector finds ready/failed work → manager reads its queue/plan → decides agent assignment or follow-up → launched lanes and later results change board/plan and manager&#39;s next turn. Parent: owner sees status/alerts → changes plan, runs/stops lane or toggles autopilot → dispatcher/manager adopt changed current work state. - Boundary reachability: Managerd and dispatch are wired into every collector pass; owner inbox, Tauri commands and plan edits are first-party, directly updating the same live plan/dispatch state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model decides which issue to start, combine or defer based on the live portfolio; deterministic plan/merge gates and load limits enforce but do not choose those priorities. Human current-control is an independent supported mode. - Evidence: manager rules/session, autopilot handoff, inbox contract, mergeq. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for shipped control paths. - Caveats: A manager decision may fail or wait despite the tools. Optional CEO audit influences management but does not replace the base task manager with a guaranteed autonomous global governor.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently check the manager&#39;s account of progress/efficiency against raw collector operational measurements and send corrective directives. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A manager can inaccurately claim progress while no lanes run, reviews are stuck, costs grow or main CI remains red; ordinary manager conversation does not expose all such evidence. - Claim being audited: Whether the manager reliably turns the plan into verified mainline work within the operator&#39;s targets. - Ordinary reporting path: Manager&#39;s own chat, inbox answers, plan/queue decisions and per-lane status reports. - Complementary access path: An opt-in separate fresh CEO model lane gets collector-computed merged/started/review-health counts, scoreboards, age of pending requests and operational CI data; it does not read manager&#39;s private memory/full transcript or accept reports as ground truth. - Independence boundary: CEO has a fresh separate model session, read-only source environment and bounded raw-data snapshot independent of the manager&#39;s claimed status; both run under the same host/provider configuration and the operator controls targets. - Who acts on findings: CEO model judges findings and sends checkable directives to the manager inbox; for budget/code/target proposals it asks the human, who decides. Next CEO runs recheck whether directives improved the measured numbers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Make its own evidence-based operational audit judgment, issue corrective policy-level manager directives and test subsequent efficacy from independent raw counters. - Decision owner: Separate model-driven CEO reviewer in an enabled first-party mode; deterministic claimcheck and stages remain support for worker validation rather than this audit owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Optional CEO schedule/force-run entry point, fresh read-only lane, collector measurement snapshot, untrusted-data delimiters, typed result validation, findings/directives records and manager inbox. - Closure path: Measured operational discrepancy → CEO judgment from fresh snapshot → model directive/proposal delivered → manager changes current handling or owner resolves proposal → next CEO pass checks effects and reopens/escalates findings. - Boundary reachability: dispatch.run calls ceo.run on every cycle with [ceo] enabled; ceo.start launches a configured model review lane, and ceo.deliver writes actual manager directives/owner proposals. This opt-in path is shipped, not the planned watchdog. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Snapshot and typed parser do not decide which finding matters or which directive to issue; the distinct CEO model owns that judgment, while worker claimcheck and ordinary tests are deterministic checks. - Evidence: CEO implementation, dispatcher wiring, CEO contract, ordinary claim checker. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for first-party independently wired audit mode; no live CEO model output was reproduced. - Caveats: CEO is off by default. Its code-based snapshot does not prove factual claims outside its observed metrics; some planned CEO watchdog/ratings functions are not shipped.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party loop develops environmental/future options and returns a strategic capability adaptation into active portfolio operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model or tooling changes, future project opportunities and emerging external constraints might change strategy, but regular manager and CEO mostly inspect current/past project performance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Compare prospective external options, select a change to organizational capability and return it to current S3; no such complete chain is established. - Decision owner: Operator/developer chooses provider/model/target configuration; manager decides present plan items and CEO inspects operational KPIs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model fallback chain, Meta summaries, research-capable lanes, plan refresh, CEO targets and autopilot proposal queue. - Closure path: CEO may propose an internal code or budget change and owner may create a task, but there is no required external-environment future-option model or adaptive strategic S4-to-S3 control loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Internal throughput monitoring, recovery, scheduling and task planning are not outside-and-then intelligence even if a manager/CEO calls them policy. - Evidence: manager decisions, CEO targets, autopilot. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for the bounded absent future function. - Caveats: A user can assign prospective market research work, but that is still a requested S1 task without evidenced strategic S4 return.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No separately closed first-party constitutional mission/ultimate-policy dilemma resolution at the project portfolio recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Human-only credentials/payment choices, agent tool safety, target budgets, review and merge policy; these are operational risk/priority bounds. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Resolve a conflict about enduring mission/ethos or ultimate authority between current operations and adaptation, and govern later operations through the decision. - Decision owner: Human owner handles personal/financial decisions and sets target values; CEO can recommend targets and manager execution policy, but not replace owner&#39;s ultimate authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Owner gate/inbox, CEO proposals/approve-decline record, fixed plan/tool/merge hooks, config.toml and planned rules. - Closure path: A CEO recommendation can become an owner approval and plan item for code/budget/targets; this closes scoped operational improvement/authorization, not a demonstrated identity-policy controversy and constitutional return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Hard rules and a human-final decision are not automatically S5; the cited proposal classes are about task, spend and productivity rather than ultimate identity of the system. - Evidence: owner gate, CEO proposal/decision lifecycle, inbox contract. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; this is bounded absence, not denial of owner&#39;s real-world governance power. - Caveats: External maintainers and project owners may adopt lasting identity policies beyond the shipped operating workflow.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/75421e2aa581887f73a20a8dc5dc598fe23e4738/assessments/ti/tirith.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 75421e2aa581887f73a20a8dc5dc598fe23e4738\">75421e2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T19:59:21+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yusa-imit/colony\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">colony</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yusa-imit/colony/tree/b1a7679ac299bf4be2e278cfb25616fac52b3365\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b1a7679ac299bf4be2e278cfb25616fac52b3365\">b1a7679 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Adaptively produce software changes and work artifacts for a claimed task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unspecified implementation steps, code reality, failing checks, rebases and local task obstacles. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose implementation, tool sequence, code fixes, testing and completion or release of the current claimed task. - Decision owner: Individual model-bearing worker operating inside Colony&#39;s task protocol. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Claude Code CLI process, isolated clone, turn prompt, MCP tools, tests, Git hub, budget gate. - Closure path: Current task/goal → worker reads code → chooses changes → checks observed result → repairs or pushes verified work → records task completion. - Boundary reachability: Colony launches real workers with first-party prompt and task tools on its standard command path; model/service availability is external. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model workers choose real next actions and interpret test feedback; the deterministic board does not write code or select solutions. - Evidence: prompts.rs#L38-L80, worker.rs, hub.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for operating ownership; no external provider-backed execution in this review. - Caveats: The coding cognition is supplied by an installed external Claude Code worker; Colony owns the protocol/orchestration that directs it, not the model internals.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: Separate code-writing Claude Code worker processes, each owning a different claimed task and isolated clone. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Competing task claims, duplicate work, conflicting changes to shared files and unmet task dependencies. - Attenuating coordination relation: Workers observe atomic claim feedback and peer messages, change their commitments, reserve dependency-blocked work, negotiate overlap and integrate only after a non-force Git rebase. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: A refused claim makes the worker choose another item; a peer mention or rejected push leads to coordination, rebase and retesting before completing its own task. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The first-party protocol connects a named inter-worker collision to its observed refusal or conflict, then instructs the model worker to change its plan and return a non-conflicting commitment. The mechanical lock alone would not suffice. - Function: Attenuate interference between parallel workers changing the same goal/repository and return conflict signals to those workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing claims, duplicate task proposals, overlapping edits, unmet dependencies and stale peer assumptions can destabilize otherwise productive S1 workers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Re-select work after a rejected claim, renegotiate task boundaries via peer chat, revise after conflicting Git changes, or reserve/release an interdependent task. - Decision owner: Peer model workers interpret the conflicting board/chat/Git feedback; board and hub provide non-discretionary exclusivity constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Atomic claim/rejection, duplicate proposal filter, dependency reservation, shared chat/notes, mid-turn inbox and non-force-push shared branch. - Closure path: Worker A claims/changes a task → worker B sees refused claim, chat mention or changed hub → B selects another task, coordinates boundaries or rebases → new board/branch state is visible to both. - Boundary reachability: Multiple workers and board tools are the normal Colony runtime, not an external application constructing its own regulator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Locks and rebases alone are automatic S2 support; positive attribution is limited to model-owned choice to replan, coordinate and repair after those feedback signals. - Evidence: prompts.rs#L46-L75, board.rs#L391-L562, inbox.rs, hub.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high; the code establishes the regulation loop, not conflict-recovery success rates. - Caveats: When only one worker is started, cross-worker S2 is dormant; low-level locking itself is not counted as agent autonomy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: Every worker reads the shared board with all open/claimed/done tasks and the common goal, rather than merely its own tool log. - Current-control decision scope: Any worker can propose missing goal work, amend present task dependencies and commitments, reopen failed completion, or close the whole goal after all current tasks finish and configured checks pass. - Function: Distribute current whole-goal task commitments and regulate unresolved work across the shared colony. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Missing goal subtasks, blocked critical paths, stale completed work, all-done-but-goal-incomplete conditions and changed availability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: From a whole-board current view, propose a 5–15-task plan, add missing work, pick/reassign one&#39;s commitment, reopen a flawed completed task, and finally declare goal completion. - Decision owner: Distributed model workers; one agent initially takes the planning task but any worker may amend the plan or exercise whole-goal closure. No separate central director exists. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: boardview, shared goal, task dependencies/status, board wakeups, one-planning-task bootstrap, goalcomplete eligibility and optional cached verifier. - Closure path: Goal/current board → worker identifies missing or blocked work → proposes/reopens and changes commitments → colleagues see the revised board and act → terminal whole-goal verification/closure. - Boundary reachability: The shipped standard protocol initializes the planning task and gives every worker whole-board tools and goal-completion rights. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The status checks are deterministic, but agents decide priorities, propose new current work and judge missing completeness; S3 is distributed rather than embodied by a manager process. - Evidence: prompts.rs#L46-L63, prompts.rs#L89-L99, mcp.rs#L188-L204, board.rs#L852-L890. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; no measured claim of effective governance across arbitrary worker counts. - Caveats: No separately privileged S3 agent; the same workers own S1 and distributed S3 choices. Operator-goal changes and budgets remain external.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Claim being audited: A peer&#39;s done task or final shared-branch state meets its acceptance criteria and the colony goal. - Ordinary reporting path: Task owner writes taskcomplete summary with a merged commit; status appears on the board. - Complementary access path: Another worker can inspect the actual shared branch and task details, execute checks, then call taskreopen with a reason; configured verify independently executes an operator-specified test command against the current hub commit. - Independence boundary: Source access and separate peer model process are available but independent reviewer assignment is optional and not enforced; automated verify is a check, not a separate agent judgment. - Who acts on findings: Reopened task becomes claimable and workers are signaled; a failed verify refuses goalcomplete, announces the failure and requires branch repair. - Function: Conditional peer challenge of task completion and reality check of the merged goal state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Misleading done summaries, broken peer changes and unverified whole-goal completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A peer can reject a completion with evidence; optional verifier can block a false goal-complete declaration. - Decision owner: A distinct worker when it undertakes peer inspection; test-command outcome and close-goal gate are deterministic. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: taskreopen, source/branch access, task logs, peer notifications, verify commit cache and closure refusal. - Closure path: Owner&#39;s done claim → separate worker inspects actual artifact → reasoned reopen → peer correction/new claim → fresh completion; optional test failure triggers the same operational repair path. - Boundary reachability: Peer-reopen tool and prompt are standard; a different reviewer agent and configured verify command are possible but not required on every completion, warranting C rather than A. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The peer can make a substantive source-grounded audit decision; generic testing/board messages are not themselves independent S3 agency. - Evidence: prompts.rs#L89-L99, board.rs#L692-L719, mcp.rs#L269-L285, mcp.rs#L420-L445. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium for conditional peer pathway; independent-review coverage not established. - Caveats: taskreopen can be used by the original worker; no source code mandates a distinct auditor. A test failure and its deterministic gate do not prove auditor independence.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No evidenced distinct prospective external scanning and organization-level capability adaptation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Current goal implementation, API usage and peer failures, not an evolving future external environment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose and propagate forward-looking capability/strategy shifts in response to environmental futures; absent. - Decision owner: Operator sets goals, worker count, tools and usage limits; workers can revise within-goal plans only. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plan task T1, peer notes, repeated sessions, usage-limit throttling and dynamic current tasks. - Closure path: Within-goal task replanning returns to present operations; no prospective option evaluation leading to durable new capability and authorized integration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Choosing tomorrow&#39;s step of an already fixed software goal is current execution, not S4 environmental adaptation. - Evidence: prompts.rs#L38-L102, supervisor.rs#L1-L14. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for reviewed first-party modes. - Caveats: A user may assign a future-oriented project goal; task subject matter does not establish a distinct harness S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No harness-owned identity/ultimate-policy governance that can legitimately redefine the binding goal and return a constitutional decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User-configured budget, deadlines, permissions and Git restrictions are operational bounds, not identity disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Resolve binding-purpose/policy conflicts with authorized parent return; absent. - Decision owner: Operator supplies goal and budget, Rust supervisor enforces stops, and workers choose within those bounds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Goal text, usage caps, task/branch gates, deterministic verifier and human external CLI configuration. - Closure path: Worker goal-complete stops the job and budget stop cancels workers; neither authorizes a change of organizational identity. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A worker&#39;s finishing declaration is current objective closure (S3), not binding S5 purpose adjudication. - Evidence: supervisor.rs, mcp.rs#L420-L445, prompts.rs#L38-L46. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Operator authorization can govern the colony from outside the reviewed first-party system; no internal legitimate parent role is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/387aebd9a81afa0b50514e12aa1899cc9771776d/assessments/co/colony.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 387aebd9a81afa0b50514e12aa1899cc9771776d\">387aebd &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T16:05:09+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MattGrundy/leadkit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Leadkit</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MattGrundy/leadkit/tree/3efcde576a5f687b0013fad902fe9cff8be11766\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3efcde576a5f687b0013fad902fe9cff8be11766\">3efcde5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement project-scoped coding and verification tasks through agent-selected reading, editing, testing and tool use. - Disturbance / variety regulated: variable code requests, test outcomes, repository content and task-specific errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the installed developer/specialist Claude Code Agent decides how to perform its assigned task and reacts to tool observations and reviewer feedback. - Decision owner: active model-driven role Agent in Claude Code Teams, configured by Leadkit&#39;s shipped first-party role definitions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Claude Code model/tool host, installed role allowlist, task files, path guard, queue/status verbs and project profile. - Closure path: tools return output to the active agent; it changes/tests code, records evidence and returns its result to task/reviewer queue. - Boundary reachability: standard adoption installs the role definitions and wires hooks; the deployed Host Agent Teams directly consumes these, whereas bare leadkit CLI-only execution is explicitly not claimed to be autonomous. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the role agent chooses substantive code edits and validation steps; CLI/guards enforce boundaries and do not generate the operational choices. - Evidence: internal/starter/agents/developer.md#L1-L40; internal/starter/agents/lead.md#L1-L42; internal/starter/starter.go#L1-L85; docs/ADOPTING-LEADKIT.md#L1-L105. - Basis: explicit / structural within adopted supported mode. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: model execution belongs to external Claude Code; no live paid Claude Code Teams E2E was performed, and disabled Teams yields no autonomous team.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: lead-assigned developer and specialist Agents working potentially overlapping git paths and successive reviews, each with its own task/owner identity. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent edits to the same path can silently undo peer work; premature parallel critique can review a SHA invalidated by prior code-review fixes; orphaned task ownership can stall delivery. - Attenuating coordination relation: lead Agent deliberately assigns path ownership, sequences the code-review and critique sessions, returns failures to original owner, and orchestrator Agent resolves stale/idle queue handoffs. Path/queue and gate checks enforce these decisions. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: reviewer findings change the owner&#39;s next implementation, a changed commit invalidates old approval, and queue/orphan status causes reassignment or escalation rather than duplicate work. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: these mechanisms target concrete shared-path collision, stale-SHA review race and lost-ownership hazards and return corrective outcomes to specific work units. - Function: attenuate interference among parallel project Agents so ownership and review outcomes remain consistent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: shared-file contention, role drift, stale review race, lost work and duplicate ownership. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Lead chooses paths and reviewer order; the orchestrator chooses how to handle queue stalls within documented roles; deterministic planner provides constraints and signals. - Decision owner: model-driven Lead/Orchestrator Agents in the shipped enabled-team operating mode, not the Go planner or filesystem alone. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ownership guard, role lanes, task IDs, deterministic plan, reviewer chain SHA binding and distinct identities. - Closure path: owner assignment enters task store/queue, agents act within scoped paths, review findings return to builder, and next verified SHA advances review/ship stages. - Boundary reachability: these roles and paths are installed through the public adoption workflow and use active Claude Code Teams Agent/SendMessage capabilities, not only project maintainer dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the meaningful assignment and remediation selection belongs to autonomous role Agents; guards deny inconsistent actions but do not choose the route. - Evidence: internal/starter/agents/lead.md#L1-L42; internal/starter/agents/orchestrator.md#L1-L28; internal/starter/team-protocol.md#L1-L100; internal/orchestrate/plan.go#L1-L130; internal/gate/chain.go#L65-L140. - Basis: explicit / structural. - Confidence: medium-high for standard deployed role protocol. - Caveats: coordination relies on following first-party role instructions; the Go CLI does not itself launch or monitor a proprietary model session.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Whole-system current view: project queue/status/owner/priority/dependency records plus live roster passed in from Agent Teams; account burn telemetry, workstream count, budget reserve and pause-lock status. - Current-control decision scope: choose active team workload and concurrency, defer dispatch when budget is stressed, prioritize critical tasks, requeue orphaned work or grant owner-directed staffing/priority changes. - Function: maintain present project-team commitments, workload and throughput within user resource and authority bounds. - Disturbance / variety regulated: misallocated role capacity, budget burn, stuck tasks and competing current priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: first-party planner exposes S3-specific route/requeue/hold/triage choices, while the installed autonomous actor must assemble a live roster and act on higher-level capacity/priority reports; in the parent mode the human selects the active roster/priority/parallelism and authorizes changes. - Decision owner: C constructor has deterministic CLI and Agent-facing role protocol but lacks a self-contained autonomous S3 governor with all decisive rights; P mode is the legitimate project operator who decides and applies current resource/priority constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: leadkit status, burn/priority planner, usage history, account pause lock, role roster, task queue, apply confirmation, waker/sweep daemons. - Closure path: computed route/hold/escalation is returned to orchestrator or human; accepted priority/roster/budget decisions alter task status or available consumers, and subsequent queue/burn samples expose the result. - Boundary reachability: CLI+roles+hooks/daemons are standard installed interfaces, including explicit owner changes, not private governance; C still needs external Agent Teams and operator adoption, P needs actual human choice. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: consuming a deterministic burn verdict and routing plan does not by itself make the autonomous Lead own a whole-system capacity tradeoff. Parent control is a distinct explicit operating mode rather than a decoration on an approval button. - Evidence: internal/orchestrate/plan.go#L1-L130; internal/orchestrate/apply.go#L1-L80; cmd/leadkit/cmdorchestrate.go#L1-L110; internal/burn/burn.go#L1-L115; internal/burn/throttle.go#L1-L70; docs/ADOPTING-LEADKIT.md#L20-L105. - Basis: explicit / structural. - Confidence: medium; project-specific actual authorization and agent roster are deployment-specific. - Caveats: automatic pause/resume is deterministic enforcement; the C notation is restricted to S3-specific live-resource decision paths, not arbitrary queue commands. Parent mode presumes a real operator uses reports to revise priority/roster rather than merely installing a static config.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: a code diff at a pinned SHA is correct/safe and actually serves the ticket&#39;s intended goal. - Ordinary reporting path: builder Agent&#39;s completion claim, code changes and test logs. - Complementary access path: separate read-only code-review and critique Agents inspect diff, task and surrounding code under different questions and record own SHA-bound verdicts/evidence. - Independence boundary: distinct installed roles/tools (reviewers lack Edit/Write/SendMessage), order and different-role gate checks; reviewers are not the builder and may not file the other layer&#39;s verdict. - Who acts on findings: original builder/lead receives changes-needed; gate blocks push/PR path until matching independent ready layers, then finalise/human may proceed. - Function: complementary audit of operational output and purpose before a consequential code shipment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: self-certified implementation errors, wrong goal, stale-SHA approval, and review-role collusion/impersonation accidents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separate model-driven reviewer Agents make substantive correctness and goal-fit judgments, each recorded on observed commit evidence. - Decision owner: reviewer Agents own their respective judgments, while Go gate and forge enforcement decide only whether required current, role-separated records exist. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewer read-only grants, mutation-evidence notes, chain/order, SHA stamps, status/queue, push hooks and optional verified server protection. - Closure path: changes-needed returns work to developer, revised SHA invalidates old approvals, both ready verdicts permit finalise/push within granted authority. - Boundary reachability: reviewer roles and gate are shipped/installable first-party components in the normal team setup, not merely CI tests; the external Claude Code host executes reviewers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: semantic findings belong to independent review Agents; checking SHA/order/identity and command gating is deterministic enforcement. - Evidence: internal/starter/agents/code-review-agent.md#L1-L65; internal/starter/agents/critique-agent.md#L1-L85; internal/gate/chain.go#L1-L140; internal/starter/team-protocol.md#L35-L100; cmd/leadkit/cmdgate.go#L1-L95. - Basis: explicit / structural. - Confidence: medium-high for first-party role separation and gate. - Caveats: recorded --by identity may be self-asserted when host does not bind CLAUDEAGENTNAME; local hooks are bypassable without forge protection. Multiple reviewers may share provider/model family; independence is role/evidence separation, not externally certified audit.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no evidenced external-future sensing, adaptation-option judgment and return into current project capability at the assessed boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: emerging technology/organizational threats or future opportunities are not a first-party recurrent object of control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party S4 actor chooses strategic redesign from external signals and deploys it. - Decision owner: not established; humans may modify roles or open issues; normal Lead coordination operates on present tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: feedback-as-issues in overarching-lead protocol, role capture, postmortems, repo governance and backlog. - Closure path: issues preserve potential improvements but no closed strategic adoption loop into team capabilities is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planning future tasks and capturing lessons is not necessarily outside-and-then adaptation. - Evidence: internal/starter/agents/overarching-lead.md#L1-L110; internal/starter/starter.go#L1-L100; README.md#L75-L145. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the user can commission tool upgrades; they remain discretionary external work, not a shipped S4 mode. ### Absence scope\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: whether a project team may grant itself standing rights to push, create PRs, merge or modify protected repository policy, versus retaining those fundamental authorization rights at the repository owner. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: direct project/repository owner alone; peer lead and overarching coordinator cannot grant or relay authority in lieu of the owner. - Return-to-operation path: owner-approved authority.&lt;repo&gt; entry in the project profile changes guards and governed PR/push commands; protected-branch rules can be installed and read back, and subsequent operations are permitted/blocked by the returned grant. - Function: reserve ultimate scope of consequential agent action to direct human owner authority and apply it as a durable team constitution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: agent self-grant, laundering authority through a peer coordinator, unreviewed merges/pushes and inconsistent rights across repositories. - Decisive decision or feedback right: grant or deny standing repository mutation/merge authority, and amend protected-branch policy under owner mandate. - Decision owner: legitimate human project/repository owner in the shipped parent-governed setup, not the autonomous Lead or other session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-repo authority profile, adoption stop point, PR/push guard, default-deny allowlists, forge protection verification and doctor. - Closure path: team must request direct owner grant; owner decision is written to profile/forge policy; subsequent hook/CLI operations consume it, permit only granted action and report denials. Denial leaves default-deny intact. - Boundary reachability: authority-grant step and policy guards are part of normal adoption flow and CLI; code explicitly reads named per-repo authority, so the mode is real without relying on GitHub contributors as a metasystem. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents cannot legitimately establish their own standing constitutional authority merely by relaying a request; the owner remains the sole source of grants, while Go enforces the returned decision. - Evidence: docs/ADOPTING-LEADKIT.md#L20-L105; internal/starter/agents/overarching-lead.md#L1-L110; README.md#L75-L145; docs/design/agent-sandboxing-and-enforcement.md#L1-L95; cmd/leadkit/cmdgate.go#L1-L95. - Basis: explicit / structural parent-governed closure. - Confidence: medium-high for first-party policy path, not for an unseen specific live owner decision. - Caveats: ordinary per-PR human review is not counted as S5; standing authority is the constitutional object. Client-side hooks alone are bypassable; host/forge enforcement and real owner-supplied profile must be configured.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a4ff63c7225d233941c9c33cd63d66584ef61ae4/assessments/le/leadkit.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a4ff63c7225d233941c9c33cd63d66584ef61ae4\">a4ff63c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-13T22:24:43+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/didi/helixharness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HelixHarness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-13\">2026-11-13</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/didi/helixharness/tree/8b23069fb464104ce556798ec69737f7eba469b3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8b23069fb464104ce556798ec69737f7eba469b3\">8b23069 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Host-agent-directed specification, implementation, testing and archiving of a concrete software feature. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing requirements, repository code/constraints, failing tests, changed review scope and incomplete task evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Host coding agent chooses feature decomposition, implementation edits, tests and review remediation within the loaded first-party workflow. - Decision owner: Codex/Claude Code agent executing shipped HelixHarness command/workflow as a supported host plugin mode; human supplies objectives and special decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Workflow router, Spec/Tasks state, lifecycle scripts, validation receipts, knowledge/context loading, TDD hooks and evidence ledger. - Closure path: Propose produces Spec/Tasks → Apply model edits code and gets tests/reviewer feedback → repair or human decision → Archive seals verified task and relevant knowledge for later features. - Boundary reachability: Public plugin commands bind to source-shipped single/workspace workflows and execute within supported Codex/Claude Code sessions; external model host supplies reasoning but first-party plugin instantiates task-specific role/procedure and closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the coding model leaves deterministic stage/receipt validators, not an owner that can implement new functionality or choose issue-specific code changes. - Evidence: helixharness/commands/propose.md, helixharness/commands/apply.md, helixharness/workflows/single/apply.md, helixharness/scripts/workflowrouter.py, docs/user-guide.md - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Coordinate distinct repository S1 workers in a multi-repository Workspace according to dependency and exclusive ownership constraints. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel repo changes can overlap ownership, consume outputs in incorrect order or race parent edits. - Distinct S1 units: Independent per-repository RepoJobs with agent-owned Propose/Apply/Archive work. - Inter-S1 disturbance: A downstream service depends on a not-yet-ready upstream service; two workers writing the same repository would collide. - Attenuating coordination relation: Workspace Coordinator constructs a dependency-aware wave, launches isolated RepoJobs with single owner per repo, waits for native terminal results and recomputes plan on validated completion or blocker. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Failure/needsinput blocks dependent successors; successful independent completion checks enable eligible following RepoJobs and update the root feature state. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The documented single-owner, dependency/forbidden-scope and validated-return rules regulate real interference between concurrent S1 code units; source scripts reject dependency cycles. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model-operated Workspace parent selects valid dispatches and revises work frontier from source-verified job outcomes. - Decision owner: First-party Workspace Coordinator role instantiated in supported coding-agent host; deterministic DAG/validation enforces bounds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Workspace registry, RepoJob contract, dependency DAG, host-native spawn/wait mapping, read/write scopes and independent completioncheck. - Closure path: Root Spec+repo graph → Coordinator waves → repo-level agent work → native terminal and source check → reconstructed plan changes later S1 dispatch. - Boundary reachability: Shipped workspace workflow and scripts create jobs and host-contract mapping, requiring a supported host-native subagent facility or documented serial unavailable fallback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static DAG sorting alone is not S2 autonomy; the bounded coordinating host agent handles actual job assignment, agent callbacks and failed dependencies. - Evidence: helixharness/reference/workspace-repo-dispatch.md, helixharness/scripts/workspace.py, helixharness/reference/subagent-runtime-protocol.md, docs/user-guide.md - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Parent oversight of current project/Workspace commitments and framework evaluation resources. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Blocked features, incomplete review evidence, mismatched dependency progress, evaluation capacity and budget contention. - Whole-system current view: Status lists active feature progress, blockers, dependencies and next steps across Workspace; optional platform shows evaluation runs, worker slots and budgets. - Current-control decision scope: Human selects which feature to continue/repair, grants scope/baseline waivers or changes the evaluation Worker pool and resource budgets. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Change present project commitments/resource constraints after examining aggregated state, rather than merely approve one code change. - Decision owner: Human developer/team or framework-maintainer parent using supported status/platform controls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Status script, Workspace plan, stage receipts, review gates, platform run/resource configuration. - Closure path: Parent views Workspace/project or platform current progress → chooses change in active work or capacity → plugin workflow or platform settings execute → next jobs/runs use changed commitment. - Boundary reachability: The plugin&#39;s status and task-update commands are shipped; optional self-hosted platform has documented UI/config and Workers. Human intervention is explicit rather than a claimed autonomous S3 manager. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: No supported agent autonomously owns allocation of the entire team&#39;s resource commitments; scripts/queues enforce the human&#39;s choices. - Evidence: helixharness/commands/status.md, helixharness/scripts/projectstatus.py, helixharness/scripts/workspace.py, docs/user-guide.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Single-feature approvals alone would not suffice; credited P concerns whole-Workspace/project status and current intervention. The optional platform has a distinct maintainer operating focus.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independent first-party code review of S1 implementation claims against direct diff, project Standards and requested Spec. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Implementer may assert tests passed while code violates standards or misses requirements. - Claim being audited: That the Apply implementation satisfies the feature Spec and repository engineering constraints. - Ordinary reporting path: Implementer completion, tests, Tasks state and verification artifacts. - Complementary access path: Frozen Review Bundle of actual git diff/working-tree scope, Spec, source Standards and independent test result; two read-only reviewer agents independently inspect the code. - Independence boundary: Standards and Spec agents are separately spawned in isolated contexts, read-only, never the Implementer context; unavailable host isolation yields BLOCKED, not self-review. - Who acts on findings: Implementer repairs MUSTFIX and runs new complete review; DECISIONNEEDED returns to human; only separate APPROVE axes enable Evidence and Archive. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Each independent reviewer agent issues reasoned evidence-backed verdict on its own axis; parent workflow accepts/rejects changes and repeats based on returned findings. - Decision owner: Two dedicated host-native subagent review instances instantiated by the first-party code-review skill; neither implementer nor deterministic checker supplies their discretionary verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Review fixed point, frozen git snapshot, source-scope hash, reviewer role contract, separate verdict logs and evidence receipts. - Closure path: Implementation → frozen actual-code evidence → independent Standards/Spec judgments → targeted repair or allowed continuation → rerun tests and review → accepted/blocked archive path. - Boundary reachability: Apply requires the shipped code-review skill and true native parallel isolated reviewers in a supported host; missing capability blocks instead of faking independence. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic scope and tests are support; two distinct review agents judge actual implementation vs independent evidence and return corrective findings. - Evidence: helixharness/skills/code-review/SKILL.md, helixharness/workflows/single/apply.md, helixharness/reference/subagent-runtime-protocol.md, helixharness/scripts/workflow.py - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: Construct a prospective framework-improvement cycle from external usage and benchmark evidence, not just current user coding work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing usage pain points, rework, capability regressions and new framework requirements outside an individual feature task. - External distinction: Usage ledger and linked telemetry from application repositories plus controlled version/Case evaluation results. - Future / prospective distinction: Improvement hypotheses and candidate commits are about what future HelixHarness framework capability should change, rather than finishing the current feature. - Adaptation option generated: Platform evolution analyzer proposes an evidence-linked hypothesis, candidate builder constructs an isolated change, and evaluation compares frozen Base/Candidate Cases. - Path back into current capability / S3: A candidate/version and its verdict are presented to a maintainer for a separate decision to publish/adopt into live plugin; the first-party platform stops short of autonomous or parent-complete production integration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Prospective option formation/evaluation is first-party and model-supported, but enduring framework adoption remains a separately composed maintainer/git workflow. - Decision owner: First-party evolution analyzer and candidate builder provide an S4-specific constructor; final source publication owner must be supplied outside this pipeline. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Telemetry consent, evidence eligibility, capability catalog, frozen Case/Run, separate Workers, scheduler and evaluated candidate ref. - Closure path: Usage/evaluation evidence → hypothesis → candidate commit → matched independent evaluation/verdict → proposed capability decision handoff; external maintainer must complete integration. - Boundary reachability: Optional installed platform exposes EvolutionService, scheduler and worker pool with documented configurations; not an imagined future extension, though disabled by default. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Option generation can be agent-driven but effective published-capability adaptation is not autonomously closed by the platform; C preserves this missing final composition. - Evidence: helixharness-platform/evolution/service.py, helixharness-platform/evolution/candidate.py, helixharness-platform/evolution/scheduler.py, docs/user-guide.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Default scheduler off; matching calibrated Cases and configured model/Workers are required. Clicking Adopt enters an experiment, not automatic publication to live plugin. No provider-backed evolution run verified.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Parent-governed team engineering mission/constraints and legitimate boundaries of what the coding harness may do. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting requests against team rules, changes to accepted knowledge, unauthorized work scopes and decisions to waive project constraints. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Which engineering principles, accepted specification, workflow rights and durable project knowledge govern the agent across tasks. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Human project owner sets binding Harness/project constraints, chooses feature scope and can confirm or refuse adding knowledge; agent cannot unilaterally redefine the enduring charter. - Return-to-operation path: Owner-approved Constraints/Spec/Knowledge documents and explicit decision receipts are persisted into application repository; later Propose/Apply reads applicable indexes and constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human maintains the standing project identity/charter and decides material changes to it rather than ordinary model-level implementation choices. - Decision owner: Project owner/team parent in the supported supervised plugin mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Document loading protocol, Spec/Tasks receipts, archive acceptance, stage/decision gates, host permissions and versioned knowledge indexes. - Closure path: Policy-level project issue → owner decision on binding requirement or durable Knowledge → accepted repository artifact → later agent workflow loads it as normative constraint and alters decisions. - Boundary reachability: The plugin&#39;s Init/Propose/Archive workflows read and write these installed project constraints; explicit owner approval gates remain part of normal supported mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent implements within frozen constraints; it does not hold legitimate ultimate authority to waive or rewrite the team&#39;s standing engineering charter. - Evidence: helixharness/reference/loading-protocol.md, helixharness/workflows/single/archive.md, helixharness/workflows/single/propose.md, docs/user-guide.md - Basis: structural -\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/50134598044ffb2248f4663fe0df5eca0f7b30ab/assessments/he/helixharness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 50134598044ffb2248f4663fe0df5eca0f7b30ab\">5013459 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T18:09:58+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ebetica/recurse\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Recurse</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ebetica/recurse/tree/6e4d231e299a14f19fe6eee9d9f7f43781780c2a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6e4d231e299a14f19fe6eee9d9f7f43781780c2a\">6e4d231 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce code/repository outcomes through independently model-directed persistent work on a task, including observation, edits, tests, terminal commands and iteration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing code state, command/test failures, incomplete context, long-running commands and interruptions/restarts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each agent chooses its subsequent tools, edits, tests, recovery, further child tasks or final result from observed feedback. - Decision owner: the Pi Durable model actor in each root/child conversation, subject to external operator authorization and safety-tool constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-agent AgentNode, Durable Harness, background shell/wait/monitor, persisted messages, working-directory state and safety checks. - Closure path: operator/parent task → model-selected tools/edits → shell/file/agent observations and completion wakes → subsequent model decision and result report; durable work can resume across browser disconnection and restart. - Boundary reachability: normal recurse /path starts a real agent; documented subagent create starts more independent model agents from CLI without a special development fixture. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model turns choose task-level actions and responses; deterministic process supervision only transports results and gates execution. - Evidence: quickstart and purpose, runtime guide, Durable turns. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: two or more independently running implementer child agents with separate model turns, local tasks and repository-editing decisions, under one parent tree. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous implementation in one checkout can overwrite, interleave or destabilize another child&#39;s in-progress file changes; the official guide explicitly motivates separate worktrees to prevent this shared-write interference. - Attenuating coordination relation: the parent model assigns separate named managed Git worktrees during subagent create --worktree, so concurrent implementers operate against isolated working files and branches; parent/worker exchange and eventual review/merge reconcile outcomes rather than expose the same working tree to competing writers. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the chosen worktree path becomes the spawned child&#39;s effective working directory for its later tool calls; its branch or PR comes back as an integration artifact, and the parent can message/reassign a child or defer/alter a merge when work overlaps. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the intervention targets a named code-edit collision between distinct S1 producers; it changes their actual workspace topology and future write targets. Messaging alone, ordinary task splitting or generic queueing is not credited. - Function: damp parallel implementation interference by deciding and maintaining task-specific checkout separation and integrating branch-level results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent editing races and inadvertent shared-workspace corruption; downstream logical Git merge conflicts remain possible. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which child receives a fresh checkout and task/branch, and adjust work allocation or subsequent merge handling after reports. - Decision owner: parent model-led agent when operating the documented parallel-worktree mode; deterministic Git/worktree holder machinery implements the isolation decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagent.ts --worktree, worktree.ts named checkouts and holder claims, per-agent cwd, Git branches, child messages/final reports. - Closure path: parent recognizes parallel edits → assigns separate worktrees → each child modifies its own checkout → branch/PR or feedback returns → parent selects review, integration, clarification or further task allocation; future child work remains separated. - Boundary reachability: the shipped CLI and runtime support worktree-bearing spawns; the first-party guide explicitly recommends and illustrates this normal multi-agent delivery pattern, not an unimplemented suggestion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent model elects when/how to separate work and which tasks/branches to assign; static worktree creation, holder bookkeeping and Git merge mechanics are enforcement, not autonomous coordinators. - Evidence: worktrees, multiagent and collision purpose, child creation interface, managed checkouts and claims, example agent graph. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: there is no automatic semantic merge-conflict arbitrator; S2 here is model-selected preventive checkout isolation and returned branch handling, not guaranteed conflict-free integration. One-agent or shared-cwd use does not exercise it.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: the parent&#39;s model-request snapshot lists its child/descendant status and queued inputs, while native subagent reads/waits/messages expose individual work and results; the supervisor maintains authoritative live membership and lifecycle status. - Current-control decision scope: the parent can choose/constrain concurrent commitments and effort on creation, interrupt or redirect active children, reassign with move, retire stale work cells and integrate or rework their output as the joint goal demands. - Function: govern a coding tree&#39;s ongoing resource/commitment allocation and exceptional intervention while independent implementers own their local tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled agents, poor distribution of effort, late user requirements, active conflicts or incomplete child tasks and divergent branch outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose whether to launch, interrupt, redirect, reparent or retire child work and whether its returned work satisfies the whole task. - Decision owner: model-backed parent agent in the supported multiagent tree; operator may intervene or take over, but operator interaction is not assumed to constitute an independent whole-team S3 parent mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: live Supervisor/tree projection, agent digest, subagent.ts create/send/interrupt/wait/move/rm, Durable report holds and completion delivery, fixed resource/process limits. - Closure path: status/child output/monitor/user steer → parent sees current tree information → parent selects task or resource intervention → supervisor/CLI changes relevant child agent and sends new input → changed work and updated status return to parent. - Boundary reachability: the agent prompt includes a live tree digest; the shipped CLI supports the control operations under normal local execution, and child reports reach the parent automatically. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent model decides interventions and current tradeoffs; the supervisor enforces ownership/fencing and routes commands but is not itself a discretionary S3 agent. Merely having a class named Supervisor is insufficient. - Evidence: parent model tree digest, CLI control surface, tree ownership and interventions, child report closure. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no first-party standard operating audit process is evidenced that independently inspects completed operational results and returns findings to current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: optimistic code-completion claims, faulty edits or unsafe commands could require independent checking. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect raw delivered artifacts outside ordinary child reporting and issue a complementary verdict with corrective return; no installed closed path established. - Decision owner: pi-safety uses a separate model to grant/deny proposed actions; an optional reviewer child can be authored by a user, but there is no default complementary outcome auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate model safety reviewer, action transcript, built-in denial gate, recipe for a review-agent graph, source tests and developer review rules. - Closure path: safety&#39;s pre-execution DENY returns a reason and can change the next actor proposal; this regulates permissions, not independent observation of the action&#39;s real outcome. User-authored review scripts may create further loops but are not established here as an active standard process. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the second model genuinely judges proposed tool authorization, but its evidence deliberately excludes tool outputs, edited file content and post-execution result; its separate model identity does not make it complementary operational audit. - Evidence: review evidence boundary and fail-open behavior, user-composed reviewer example, agent report path. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no distinct prospective environment-model and capability adaptation loop is established beyond ordinary coding tasks and event-driven continuation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing provider/tool ecosystems, CI trends, new project requirements and future architectural possibilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose a new whole-harness capability based on external/future distinctions and carry it back into live operation; not evidenced. - Decision owner: agents can investigate and change repository code when tasked, while users/developers configure tools and skills; no autonomous organization-level S4 owner is shipped. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: background monitors, memory, MCP configuration, skills, models and task-local planning. - Closure path: a CI/deployment monitor can wake an agent for immediate handling, but a signal-to-task reaction is not itself prospective option formation and a capability-adaptation return to S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planning future code steps is S1 task choice; Recurse&#39;s extensibility offers means to construct S4 but no first-party defined future/environment adaptation decision loop. - Evidence: monitor and goals examples, extensibility principles, runtime guide. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudicator is established for the multiagent coding organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: risky commands, operator intent conflicts, policy exceptions, agent takeovers and security-mode choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: settle a whole-organization identity or ultimate-policy tension and return a binding new rule; no closed function-specific path shown. - Decision owner: operator/developer owns Recurse permissions and safety instructions; model safety reviewer applies predefined action policy, and child/parent agents follow ordinary delegated task authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: user task prompts, first-party pi-safety and custom additive policy rules, operator-only takeover, tool denial circuit breaker and user consent for exact actions. - Closure path: model reviewer may return DENY and request a safer action or explicit authorization; that decides an individual command under existing policy, not a new identity-governing rule for the organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model can reason about dangerous operations, but ultimate authority to change policy is not delegated to a live S5 body with a policy-level issue/escalation and operational return. - Evidence: safety review limits, operator takeover, takeover state/authority. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e94effaef30205f31fb915634491cbab971ca89d/assessments/re/recurse.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e94effaef30205f31fb915634491cbab971ca89d\">e94effa &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T18:07:29+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/CJVigil/Orco\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Orco</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/CJVigil/Orco/tree/148f21d5a4dcf4f833e87d9fa38d3a1bfbe9df59\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 148f21d5a4dcf4f833e87d9fa38d3a1bfbe9df59\">148f21d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform substantive software/project tasks through configured model-bearing connected agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different user requirements, source code state, failing tool results, changed ticket context and incomplete information. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose investigations, tool invocations, file modifications, responses, task completion and when to ask for input. - Decision owner: Connected model agent using the launched CLI and MCP tools; the human initiating work owns the original request, not the agent&#39;s operational choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task claim leases, connected bridge, model CLI adapter, prompt/context builder, local workspace, MCP task state and policy/budget gates. - Closure path: User/other agent requests work → bridge claims task and launches model → model reads context/uses tools and adjusts work to observed results → updatetask done/blocked/failed updates shared state. - Boundary reachability: The shipped orco connect bridge actually starts configured local models from assigned tasks; the default bridge prompt and installed Claude-code skill prescribe this task-to-action lifecycle, rather than relying on the maintainer&#39;s development agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic orchestration delivers requests and enforces limits; the model chooses substantive work under those limits. - Evidence: bridge default/runner, model adapter, task API. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high for wired agent decision path; medium for behavior of the external model runtime. - Caveats: No live external model credentials or end-to-end provider sessions were exercised in this review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Prevent parallel connected operational agents from modifying an incompatible shared resource at the same time, with feedback for changed agent behavior. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two simultaneously working agents both attempt to use the same branch, deployment target, migration or another named shared project resource. - Decisive decision or feedback right: On resource conflict, choose wait, release/retry later or pick another task; decide when the shared resource can be relinquished. - Decision owner: The involved model-bearing task agents in the shipped bridge prompt / integration skill. Deterministic lock service owns exclusion and fencing, not the discretionary response to denied access. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Project-scoped named lock holder, TTL, monotonically advanced fence, conflict response identifying holder and expiry, blocking wait helper and explicit acquire/release MCP tools. - Closure path: Agent A obtains named lock → agent B sees conflict identifying holder/expiry → shipped model guidance makes B wait or change work rather than collide → lock expiry/release and subsequent acquire permit resumed work. - Boundary reachability: The bridge&#39;s bundled DEFAULTPROMPT explicitly requires acquirelock/releaselock before touching shared resources; the distributed Claude-code Orco skill explicitly specifies waiting or picking different work after a conflict. Both are provided for ordinary supported agent connections. - Distinct S1 units: Separate connected coding agents with independent task assignments and local model processes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Simultaneous work on a single branch, deployment target or migration. - Attenuating coordination relation: Mutual-exclusion leases plus a returned lock-conflict signal that the model uses to alter its next activity. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The denied model changes task/order or waits until the holder releases, so following tool actions use a nonconflicting shared resource. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The named resource collision is concrete inter-agent interference; first-party lock acquisition, conflict disclosure and instructed behavioral change close a specific attenuation cycle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Lock arithmetic and fencing are runtime enforcement, whereas agents choose the amended work schedule after feedback. - Evidence: locks, bridge prompt, installed Orco skill. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high for standard connected agents and actual conflict return. - Caveats: Lock discipline is advisory for code execution outside Orco; a noncompliant agent can bypass these project-scoped locks.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: View current work commitments and failures across managed agents/projects and change live allocation, task priorities and intervention decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Stalled agent tasks, blocked tickets, overlapping priorities, waiting approvals, CI failure, overspend and missed current commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide which current work to reprioritize, pause, assign, unblock or escalate in light of portfolio/project state. - Decision owner: Base constructor mode: a separately configured model supervisor asked by the operator to use the shipped projectplan, listtickets and updateticket tools (C); no such supervisor is installed by default. Parent mode: authenticated human org/project administrator viewing portfolio health and changing tickets/pauses/budgets (P). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Server-generated project plan and ticket summaries, cross-project health/roadmap/CI/approval/budget aggregation, ticket mutation API, scoped pause/budget control and audit events. - Closure path: Base: constructed model supervisory session reads whole project open work and plan → decides reassignment/priority update → first-party ticket edits change connected agents&#39; subsequent commitments. Parent: portfolio displays blocked/stalled/over-budget agents and tasks → admin decides assignment/priority/pause → persisted controls/tickets alter later bridge task starts and available work. - Boundary reachability: First-party MCP ships the necessary project-wide read/update tools for a constructor-configured agent mode, while the first-party Orco human UI and controls route expose portfolio current state and authorized admin changes in normal self-hosted deployment; neither requires maintainer CI or a private service. - Whole-system current view: For the base constructor, the accessible plan, tickets and dependencies of the selected Orco project (not a complete cross-instance view); for the parent, Portfolio.overview aggregates all visible projects&#39; tickets, stalled agents, approvals, CI, budgets and milestone risk. - Current-control decision scope: Assigning/updating task commitments, priority and workflow status, pausing selected agents/projects or all agents, and modifying operational budget limits in response to present problems. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The runtime computes health and enforces controls but does not select a supervisory response. An operator must construct an agent manager to own that discretionary S3 right; the shipped human-admin mode closes it without an autonomous manager. - Evidence: portfolio, MCP project/ticket actions, tickets, runtime controls. - Basis: structural + explicit - Confidence: medium for constructor eligibility, high for human-admin closure. - Caveats: C does not assert a first-party always-on autonomous supervisor. The portfolio view itself is informational until a legitimate admin/model decision changes work.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: A distinct connected review agent independently evaluates another agent&#39;s concrete work and returns corrective findings. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A coding agent may declare a ticket complete despite defects or missing acceptance requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Review the actual ticket/workspace and choose approved or changesrequested with an explanatory note. - Decision owner: A separately addressed model-bearing reviewer, not the original task agent. Human reviewers are also supported, but not used to upgrade agent ownership. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Requester != reviewer identity check, separate reviewer task and bridge wake, review status persistence, ticket review/rework transition and task restart. - Closure path: Task agent requests review of ticket → separate reviewer agent receives a new explicit task to inspect the work and submit verdict → changesrequested writes review note, moves ticket to rework and starts original agent&#39;s work again; an agent approval records assessment while final done remains gated to a person. - Boundary reachability: Reviews.request creates a real task for a named agent reviewer through first-party Tasks.create, which the shipped connected bridge executes; Reviews.submit directly implements rework and restart. - Claim being audited: Work correctness and acceptance for a particular Orco task/ticket. - Ordinary reporting path: Worker submits updatetask done or ticket progress in its own thread. - Complementary access path: A different agent&#39;s model run has a separate task identity, can read the project ticket/context and inspect actual workspace through its own configured coding CLI. - Independence boundary: Reviewer cannot be the requester; it holds a different Orco participant identity and process. Institutional independence, different model provider and read-only file permissions are not guaranteed. - Who acts on findings: The review subsystem moves the ticket to rework and starts the assigned coding agent again with the review note attached. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A reviewing LLM exercises the verdict; deterministic review/ticket machinery transmits the judgment and restarts work. - Evidence: reviews request, verdict and restart, bridge, ticket review path. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high for optional first-party reviewer mode. - Caveats: Review is not mandatory for every task. A separate agent identity alone does not prove critical rigor or provider independence; review quality is unmeasured here.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No shipped closed cycle that senses external/prospective change, selects adaptation to future capability and returns that selection to current operational repertoire. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future milestone risk, new follow-up work noticed during tasks and recurring job demand are recorded, but these are not independently established as future environment intelligence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Judge external/future environmental alternatives and adopt a changed capability or operating repertoire; not established. - Decision owner: Humans manage milestones, accept follow-up suggestions and approve schedules; ordinary connected agents can propose work and schedules but do not hold a shipped adaptation mandate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Portfolio cross-project roadmap, dated milestones, agent suggestions with optional owner-authorized ticket filing, scheduled recurring tasks and human approvals. - Closure path: Agent can suggest a follow-up → owner accepts into backlog (or an allowed automatic ticket is filed), and scheduled tasks can later run; no documented installed step converts future outside-environment analysis into a selected capability adaptation that returns to operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Backlog intake, forecast risk labels and repeat execution do not alone imply outside-and-then strategy or a governed capability change. - Evidence: suggestions, schedules, portfolio roadmap, MCP milestone operations. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium, bounded to the shipped first-party standard distribution. - Caveats: A human organization using Orco can implement strategic analysis externally; no universal claim against that larger system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Legitimate human parent authority establishes organization-wide and project-specific enduring operating identity/rules and returns binding decisions to subsequent agent work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting agent freedoms, project-local exceptions, unsafe spending/deploy authority and mismatch between organizational mandate and project execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Define or revise authoritative organizational/project rules, required approvals, allowed operational agents and persistent task-budget policy; subordinate projects may tighten but may not relax org constraints. - Decision owner: Authenticated human organization administrator or project administrator; first-party Policies.set refuses agents and applies scope checks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persisted org/project policy, mergePolicy inheritance/tightening, agent bridge prompt policy lines, new-task admission and budget validation, audit trail and controls event. - Closure path: Admin receives governance need and sets org/project policy → it is persisted and scoped into the effective project mandate → new task creation rejects forbidden agents/excess budgets; later bridge prompts include binding rules and approval requirements → subsequent agent operation reflects the decision. - Boundary reachability: The first-party self-hosted server exposes Policies.set to authenticated administrators and directly calls effective policy at task admission and rulesFor in connected-agent contexts. This path is shipped, not an assumed external user-written AGENTS.md. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Which agents and actions the ongoing project/organization authorizes, which approvals remain nondelegable and what spending or time rights every new agent task receives. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: In the one credited parent mode, org administrator sets binding server-wide rules and project administrator sets only narrower local rules; the policy setter rejects model agents outright. - Return-to-operation path: Stored administrator decision is combined into effective policy; new-task admission enforces the eligible agents and budget caps, and subsequent connected-agent task context receives policy rules and approval requirements. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Only a legitimate human admin has authority to change the continuing constitution; agents execute within its returned constraints. Discretionary governance remains at the parent recursion. - Evidence: policies, bridge context and rules, task admission. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high for hierarchy/admission/prompt return; lower for compliance with textual rules by an external model. - Caveats: Plain-language policy obedience depends on provider behavior; allowed agent lists and budget caps have stronger deterministic enforcement.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/670ef2c13fe29c9a7f414b48b2182b4727b90970/assessments/or/orco.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 670ef2c13fe29c9a7f414b48b2182b4727b90970\">670ef2c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T18:46:21+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/splashxmoon/friday\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Friday</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/splashxmoon/friday/tree/7db025c6513eae1c05a93f6e7ba58fd5ffad2a2a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7db025c6513eae1c05a93f6e7ba58fd5ffad2a2a\">7db025c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform an operator&#39;s work objective into code, tests, documents or other concrete repository artifacts and correct defects according to tool/source feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous requirements, codebase differences, unexpected test results and task-local implementation choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the assigned model-driven lane chooses substantive implementation/tool calls, interprets outcomes and revises its own artifact before reporting completion. - Decision owner: a Friday-managed coding-agent lane invoking its configured external Claude Code, Codex, other CLI or Friday Code model; not the UI, deterministic task allocator or model vendor&#39;s unrelated organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session state, local CLI adapter and worktree, role instructions, checkpoints, task board, test runner, manual permission mode and event history. - Closure path: human/lead task → owned lane executes substantive model/tool steps → workspace and check feedback → revised files/report → task complete or returned for rework. - Boundary reachability: README standard Code and Crew paths, src/runner/runner.ts run/turn interface, src/agent-mesh.ts assign/claim/finish and src/teammates.ts role handoff are shipped first-party integration paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-driven lane leaves only launch, log and gate code; that code cannot determine source-specific implementation by itself. - Evidence: pinned README, src/runner/runner.ts, src/agent-mesh.ts, src/teammates.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for owned role and executable paths, not for every vendor CLI combination&#39;s observed quality. - Caveats: provider CLI agents and models remain external execution dependencies; their internal agent hierarchies are not attributed to Friday.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate interference among simultaneously editing worker lanes by assigning file custody and returning conflict warnings to the agent before further edits. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two otherwise independent agent lanes claiming or editing the same file, incompatible interface changes and stale board/task ownership. - Decisive decision or feedback right: accept non-overlapping claimfiles paths; reject claims already owned by other lanes; on a conflicting diff, instruct the colliding worker to stop editing and consult or wait for the holder. - Decision owner: constructor-defined Friday mesh claim/ownership checks; subsequent peer negotiation is by task actors, not evidence of a dedicated autonomous S2 policy authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: files.held, files.released, board owns sets, heldBy, onDiff, agent messages and wake events. - Closure path: first lane holds path → second lane claims or changes it → Friday identifies holder and refuses claim or warns the violator → second lane must stop and coordinate or wait before continuing. - Boundary reachability: agent-visible claimfiles and releasefiles tools and automatic onDiff are part of shipped src/agent-mesh.ts integration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coordination relation reaches worker behavior, but collision arbitration is a prewritten deterministic rule; peer dialogue alone does not prove an independent S2 agent making the binding priority policy. - Distinct S1 units: independent Friday-managed coding-agent lanes working in the same session/repository. - Inter-S1 disturbance: conflicting writes/claims to the same source path or a board-owned file. - Attenuating coordination relation: ownership claim, refused second claim and holder-specific warning on changed files. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: collision message explicitly directs the second worker to stop changing the file, ask the owner, or wait for release. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: it detects and returns a concrete cross-worker collision, rather than merely listing agents or assigning tasks. - Evidence: pinned src/agent-mesh.ts (holdFiles, releaseFiles, onDiff, heldBy) and README team-worktree instructions. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for the implemented narrow path. - Caveats: collision warning and cooperative file claiming are advisory; no proof that arbitrary external tools cannot violate the instructions or that the entire repository has transactional file locks.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: an authorized lead agent manages present board commitments, assignment, current blocked work, reallocation and completion under an operator&#39;s goal. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled team progress, uneven available capacity, failed checks/review, agents running out of usage, inconsistent task progress and unclear priorities across active lanes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the designated lead model can autonomously create/decompose a board, assign work, steer or stop a teammate in Crew mode, cancel/retry/reassign blocked tasks, inspect reports and decide the next current step. - Decision owner: the configured first-party-managed lead agent inside a Friday Code/Crew session; Friday enforces role permissions, and the human owner still has higher-level intervention rights. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: guarded posttasks, assigntask, managetask, steeragent; dependency/cycle checks, finite board, event projection, current budget/usage signals, deterministic dispatch, retries and lead reports. - Closure path: goal/current workload and progress evidence → lead model posts or adjusts the board/steers worker → Friday dispatches its authorized decision → reports/tests/usage/blocks return to lead → lead revises assignments or calls for remediation. - Boundary reachability: src/agent-mesh.ts offers board management to the lead and reserves steering to state.lead; src/team-selfrun.ts explicitly tells an enabled autonomous lead to choose the next valuable tasks, hand them out, decide open questions, and stop only at goal completion/budget/stall. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current-work authority is not only deterministic queue management; an explicitly designated model-driven lead can choose and alter task partition and reprioritization through role-gated tools. This positive state requires the supported lead/Crew path, not passive solo-agent UI mode. - Whole-system current view: one session/room&#39;s board statuses, owners, checks, message reports, agent availability, usage limits and budget, bounded by the human&#39;s goal. - Current-control decision scope: board admission and redistribution, teammate redirection and when to try/stop/rework; not a demonstrated corporate-level resource or identity authority. - Evidence: pinned src/agent-mesh.ts (manageTask, postTasks, control, steer, round, reportBoard) and src/team-selfrun.ts; README Crew path. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for first-party-owned agent lead control mode. - Caveats: whether the chosen provider model actually exercises that discretion well is not observed; deterministic budgets and automated selection are C components within this A ownership claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: ask a separate model-driven reviewer to challenge a worker&#39;s own completion claim against its actual artifacts, task requirements and recorded proof, and return necessary corrections before board completion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unverified self-reported completion, missed requirements and defects not caught by the worker&#39;s own tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a different available reviewer lane reads another agent&#39;s task, report, proof and diff/source and returns a substantive approval or correction verdict. - Decision owner: the independently assigned model-driven reviewer lane, distinct from the task&#39;s implementing lane. Friday&#39;s check runner and verdict parser are support/gates rather than the substantive audit judge. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewOrPass, different-lane pickReviewer, awayDiff, proof/changes handoff, board.reviewed, bounded rework, configured tests, optional model-based goalRequest judge. - Closure path: worker reports finished task + actual diff/proof → Friday runs its checks → if eligible, separate reviewer sees task and evidence → reviewer decides pass or correction → failed verdict sends defects back to worker and repeats review, or terminal escalation after rework bound. - Boundary reachability: the standard multi-agent board review path src/agent-mesh.ts dispatches to a lane other than t.lane; onRunEnded records its verdict, and rework returns rejected findings. A separate task goal judge is available under src/goal.ts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in the supported second-review path, the reviewer model reads changed artifacts and makes a case-specific challenge that code cannot predefine. Automatic checks alone, a UI read-only panel, and an operator&#39;s final review are not counted as autonomous S3. - Claim being audited: a specific assigned board task is correctly finished and fits acceptance/requirements. - Ordinary reporting path: implementing lane&#39;s completion report, test commands and worktree changes. - Complementary access path: Friday presents the other reviewer source/worktree or the recorded diff and task proof, with independent reviewer instructions and test receipts rather than merely trusting the author&#39;s summary. - Independence boundary: different agent lane, not guaranteed different model vendor or fully isolated model context; source access depends on whether the reviewer runs on the same machine or receives a recorded diff. - Who acts on findings: mesh sends failed verdict and concrete instructions back to the original task lane for bounded repair; successful review unlocks dependent tasks. - Evidence: pinned src/agent-mesh.ts (runChecks, reviewOrPass, pickReviewer, round, boardRunEnded, rework, awayDiff), src/team-proof.ts, src/goal.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high in configured multi-agent second-review mode. - Caveats: Friday deliberately skips cross-review for qualifying small changes with real passing tests, no changed files or unavailable alternate reviewer; thus S3=A is conditional, not universal. Vendor/model or context independence is not proven beyond different lanes and evidence paths.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no demonstrated first-party decision loop that turns anticipated external developments into changes of the Friday work organization&#39;s future mission/capability envelope. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changed tool limits, learned model effectiveness, later repo errors and new scheduled tasks are sensed, but the cited response is route optimization or current execution within existing permissions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model/tool selection, replayed lessons and human-accepted reusable skills do not by themselves constitute durable first-party prospective-capability choice. - Decision owner: constructor-defined scoring rules and human skill approver for the observed adaptation mechanisms; no bounded autonomous Friday S4 organizational actor identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: route-learning feedback grades, model benchmark priors, routing.ts choice, Crew skill proposals and human acceptance, memory/automation setup. - Closure path: observed test/review feedback → model-fit estimate/current routing or proposed skill → later present task; no environmental forecast and S4 option evaluation leading to an authorized changed future organizational capability is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a feedback-driven heuristic and task-level learning are not enough to assign future-facing organization intelligence; even autonomous self-run leads act on the current user goal and board. - Evidence: pinned src/route-learning.ts, src/routing.ts, src/team-skills.ts, src/team-selfrun.ts, README memory/automation sections. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: this is not a denial of ordinary learning, future-oriented user projects, or the possibility that a parent organization uses Friday as its S4 tool.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no supported ultimate-identity or constitutional-policy adjudication loop for a Friday installation was found. - Disturbance / variety regulated: access modes, permissions, publishing authority, spending/usage bounds and goal text arrive as user-set constraints or constructor safeguards rather than deliberated organization policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the person establishes the goal, access level, budgets, and final commit/PR/deployment approval; model-driven workers cannot legitimize a change of identity or waive these user boundaries. - Decision owner: external local user/owner and platform permission constraints; no first-party S5 policy-maker with a demonstrated return to the running organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Crew read/ask/full permissions, src/team-budget.ts, approval events, self-run prohibitions on default-branch push/deployment/data deletion/spending, manual final review. - Closure path: human chooses constraints → Friday enforces them during tasks → agent operates in the bounded space; no question of ultimate mission/identity is assessed by an S5 authority and promulgated as a legitimate new policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an autonomous lead controlling work is S3, not by itself a constitutional S5, and static access/approval settings are control tools rather than a policy arbitration loop. - Evidence: pinned README human review/Crew settings, src/teammates.ts, src/team-selfrun.ts, src/team-budget.ts, src/runner/runner.ts. - Basis: bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a human operator or organization may exercise S5 outside the shipped runtime; such parent authority is not silently imported.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d1c43df574e84c7b5d9a3e8b68ab4ea0be83d739/assessments/fr/friday.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d1c43df574e84c7b5d9a3e8b68ab4ea0be83d739\">d1c43df &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
